/* Bespoke narrative for each family page. Kept in its own module because
   Astro calls getStaticPaths() before the page frontmatter is evaluated,
   so a `const` declared there is not yet defined when the paths are asked for. */
export const COPY = {
  blazevic: {
    eyebrow: "Smokvica Krmpotska, then Senj",
    dek: "The name the archive carries. Eight generations, from a boy born about 1750 in a Bunjevac settlement to a woman who died in Johannesburg — and the parish register that holds the first five of them has never been opened.",
    body: [
      ["The hinge is one man coming off the mountain", "<p>Every Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; ancestor on the direct line before 1892 is placed at <strong>Smokvica Krmpotska</strong>, a village in the Vinodol hills behind Novi Vinodolski &mdash; founded, like <a href='/prpic'>Krivi Put</a>, by <strong><a href='/bunjevci'>Bunjevci Krmpo&cacute;ani</a></strong> settled there by the Military Frontier between 1605 and 1647.</p><p><a href='/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic'>Toma Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;</a> (1838&ndash;1907) is the hinge. He is born at <strong>house 114 in Smokvica Krmpotska</strong> and he dies at <strong><a href='/senj'>Senj</a></strong>, a <em>nadni&ccaron;ar</em> &mdash; day labourer. <strong>The family comes off the mountain into a port town in the decades <em>after</em> that port stopped working.</strong></p>"],
      ["His death entry, read", "<p>The Senj death register for 1907, p. 225, entry 60, gives him in full.</p><blockquote class='note'><strong>26 <em>srpnja</em> &mdash; July &mdash; 1907</strong><br /><strong>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; Toma, <em>nadni&ccaron;ar</em></strong> &middot; <strong><em>udovac iza pok. Tereze</em></strong> &mdash; widower of the late Tereza<br />aged <strong>69</strong>, of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>, sacraments administered<br /><span class='dek'>buried at the cemetery of <strong>sv. Vid</strong> on 28 July</span></blockquote><p><strong>He is not alone on that page.</strong> Two entries above him a tobacco-factory worker of thirty dies of the same disease, three days earlier; three entries above, a coachman of twenty-four is entered as <em>ustrieljen</em> &mdash; <strong>shot</strong> &mdash; with the sacraments <em>not</em> administered. <strong>This is one week in one small town.</strong></p>"],
      ["House 114 is a zadruga", "<p>The register identifies a household by its house number, and one number runs through this family. <a href='/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic'>Toma</a> is born at <strong><em>Br. 114, Smokvica Krmpotska</em></strong> in 1838, and is recorded there with Terezija Pe&cacute;ani&cacute; at the baptisms of <strong>Ivan in 1882</strong> and <strong>Tonka in 1884</strong>. In <strong>1895</strong> <a href='/people/juraj-georgius-jure-blazevic'>Juraj</a> appears in the same book as <strong>&laquo;Jurko Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; i Tereza ro&dstrok;. &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;, <em>selj.</em>&raquo;</strong>, at <strong>Smokvica kbr. 114</strong>.</p><div class='fix'><h3>This archive first read that as one household, and was wrong</h3><p>A control document settles it: the baptism of <strong>Frane Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;, 2 August 1890</strong>, puts <strong>Pave Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; i Marija ro&dstrok;. Tomljanovi&cacute;</strong> at <strong>Smokvica 114</strong> as well &mdash; and that Pavao is the son of <strong>Luka</strong>, not of Toma. <strong>Three distinct nuclear families share the number across thirteen years.</strong></p></div><p>They join one generation up. <strong>Luka Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;</strong> and <a href='/people/anton-blazevic'>Anton Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;</a>, Toma's father, are both sons of <a href='/people/ilija-blazevic'>Ilija Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;</a>. Pavao and Toma are <strong>first cousins</strong>. Smokvica 114 is therefore a <strong><em>zadruga</em></strong> &mdash; the South Slav extended-family household, in which married brothers and cousins live under one number and hold the land in common. It is the characteristic form of the Military Frontier and of the <a href='/bunjevci'>Bunjevac settlements</a> &mdash; and <a href='/kalanj'>Klenovica</a> turns out to have been organised the same way.</p><p>So the number places Juraj inside <strong>Ilija's kin-group</strong>, which is real and worth having, and it does <strong>not</strong> single out which man in that group was his father.</p>"],
      ["A caution about the surname", "<p>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; is <strong>the 17th most common surname in Croatia</strong> &mdash; 7,216 bearers there, 11,163 worldwide &mdash; and its densest counties are Split-Dalmatia, Virovitica-Podravina and Osijek-Baranja, <strong>none of which is this family's</strong>.</p><p><strong>Shared surname is not evidence of shared descent here</strong>, and on this page more than any other that has to be said out loud. It is the exact opposite of <a href='/zubrinic'>&Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;</a>, where the rarity of the name is itself an argument. <a href='/name'>The names &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. The parish, which is named and unopened.</strong> Toma's death entry gives his birthplace not as a village but as a parish: <strong><em>Sv. Jakov Krmpote</em></strong> &mdash; St James of Krmpote. That is the register that holds Smokvica Krmpotska, and therefore the book in which <a href='/people/juraj-georgius-jure-blazevic'>Juraj</a>, <a href='/people/anton-blazevic'>Anton</a>, <a href='/people/ilija-blazevic'>Ilija</a>, <a href='/people/grgica-blazevic'>Grgica</a> and <a href='/people/matesa-blazevic'>Mate&scaron;a</a> should be found.</p><div class='note'><strong>Nobody has opened it.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>Five of the eight generations on the line this archive is named for sit in one unread book, and its name has been in hand since the death entry was read. <a href='/worklist'>The work list &rarr;</a></span></div><p><strong>2. Which man in Ilija's kin-group fathered Juraj.</strong> The house number places him in the group and cannot go further; only a baptism can. <a href='/open-questions'>Open questions &rarr;</a></p><p><strong>3. Terezija Pe&cacute;ani&cacute;, and the &laquo;pok. Tereza&raquo; of 1907.</strong> Toma dies a widower of a Tereza; the baptisms of 1882 and 1884 give him a Terezija Pe&cacute;ani&cacute;. <strong>Her death has not been found</strong>, and it is in the same Senj register that gave his.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  zubrinic: {
    eyebrow: "Otočac, and then the world",
    dek: "The rarest name in the archive and therefore the most useful one — and the least resolved: 422 records, 90 loose ends, and a register that turned out to record three Frontier offices the family tree has never carried.",
    body: [
      ["The rarest name here, and the least resolved", "<p>About <strong>312 people on earth</strong> carry this surname, a quarter of the Croatian ones in Lika-Senj. In an archive where <a href='/blazevic'>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;</a> is the seventeenth commonest name in Croatia, that rarity is worth more than any other family's size: a &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; in a register in this district is probably kin.</p><p><strong>And the tree makes the least of it of any family here.</strong> The &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; material is not one family but <strong>422 records resolving into 90 loose ends</strong>, with <strong>92 people held together by nothing but an A&ndash;Z index</strong> &mdash; eighteen nodes whose entire given name is a single letter. Elsewhere they hang off nodes whose names are a researcher's notes; one reads <em class='mono'>&laquo;Brothers of Casparus (It Seems) &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;&raquo;</em>.</p><div class='note'><strong>So this page is about what the registers gave back</strong> when the tree was set aside and the Oto&ccaron;ac books were opened. They gave back a spelling, a rank, and a social world the family tree does not record anywhere. <a href='/zubrinic-roots'>The alphabet nodes, in full &rarr;</a></div>"],
      ["Three alphabets, one name — and here is the proof", "<p>Nothing could be searched for until the spelling was settled. The family is written <strong>Xubrinich</strong> in the Venetian-Italian convention where <em>X</em> spelled the sound <em>&Zcaron;</em>; <strong>Zubrinich</strong> in the German-Latin administrative hand; and <strong>&Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;</strong> in modern Croatian after Gaj's reform. That was an argument until the Oto&ccaron;ac register was read, and the priest settled it himself.</p><blockquote class='note'><strong>Oto&ccaron;ac, 24 January 1834</strong> &mdash; the baptism of Nicolaus<br />the father's name, written and then <strong>underlined by the priest</strong>: <strong><em>Micha&euml;l XUBRINICH</em></strong><span class='dek' style='display:block'>One hand, one entry, both alphabets acknowledged.</span></blockquote><p>That single underlining is why every search since has been run three ways. <a href='/name'>How to search it &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["And the same entry says what they were", "<p>The 1834 entry is kept in Latin, and it gives the household's <em>conditio</em> in one word: <strong><em>Confiniarius</em></strong> &mdash; a man of the <strong>Military Frontier</strong>, the militarised border the Habsburgs ran against the Ottomans, in which every household owed service. It places them <em>ex Pago Shumechia N&deg; 24</em> &mdash; the village of <strong>&Scaron;ume&cacute;ica</strong>, house 24, outside Oto&ccaron;ac, itself a Frontier regimental seat.</p><p><strong>And the godparents say it twice over.</strong> The godfather is <strong>Petrus, son of Francis Xubrinich</strong>, entered as <em>Vigiliarum Ma[gister]</em> &mdash; master of the watch &mdash; and <strong>pensioned</strong>. The godmother is <strong>Maria, wife of Joannes Xubrinich, <em>Silvarum Custos</em></strong> &mdash; <strong>forest warden</strong> at &Scaron;ume&cacute;ica. <strong>Three &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; households in one entry, each holding an office.</strong></p><div class='note'>This is a different social world from the <a href='/blazevic'>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;i</a>, who are day labourers, or the <a href='/papic'>Papi&cacute;i</a>, who carry sacks. <strong>Nothing in the family tree records any of it.</strong></div>"],
      ["The godparent column is a second source, and nobody had read it", "<p>Read on 13 September 2026, for the first time. At the baptism of <strong>Josephus &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; on 16 March 1845</strong> the godparents are <strong><em>Vitus Kranj&ccaron;evi&cacute;, murarius</em></strong> &mdash; a mason &mdash; and <strong><em>Maria &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;, mulier Custodis Carcerum, hujates</em></strong>: <strong>the wife of the prison keeper</strong>, of this place.</p><p>That matters beyond the two names. The argument above rests on the <em>parents'</em> column and the 1834 entry; this is the same claim arriving from a <strong>different column of a different year</strong>, and it adds an office &mdash; <em>Custos Carcerum</em> &mdash; held in a &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; household. <strong>The Latin Oto&ccaron;ac registers give every godparent a trade or a rank</strong>, and that column had never been read. <a href='/searched'>What has been searched &rarr;</a></p><div class='note'><strong>A note on the leaf itself.</strong> The 1834 page is torn and has been conservation-mounted; the scan carries the archive's own label, <em>Repaired Document / Restaurirani dokument</em>. Whole columns of neighbouring entries are lost to the tear. <strong>That this line survives legibly is luck.</strong></div>"],
      ["Where they are from", "<p><strong>Oto&ccaron;ac</strong> &mdash; a <a href='/frontier'>Military Frontier regimental town</a> in the Gacka valley in Lika, inland from Senj across the Velebit. <a href='/people/tereza-zubrinic-zubrinic'>Tereza &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;</a> (1867&ndash;1931), Hedviga's grandmother, is born there, as are her father <a href='/people/nicolaus-zubrinic-xubrinic'>Nicolaus</a> (1834) and grandfather <a href='/people/michael-zubrinic-zubrinic-xubrinich'>Michael</a> (1811). Above them the tree reaches <a href='/people/toma-zubrinic'>Toma &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;</a>, born 1768 at <strong>&Scaron;ume&cacute;ica 2</strong> &mdash; two doors from the house 24 the 1834 entry names.</p>"],
      ["The Australians", "<p>About 1884 <strong>Ivan &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;</strong> (1860&ndash;1930) reached <strong>Port Pirie, South Australia</strong>, and from his first child in 1885 the family is registered as <strong>Zubrinich</strong> &mdash; through Broken Hill, Whyalla and Quorn to this day. <strong>His father was a Forest Ranger at Oto&ccaron;ac</strong>, which is the same office the 1834 godmother's husband held, and his grandparents both died in <strong>1845</strong>.</p><p><strong>Our line and the Australian line are not joined by anything</strong>, though their earliest men are born a year apart in the same district. <a href='/frontier'>The Frontier &rarr;</a> &middot; <a href='/port-pirie'>Port Pirie &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Whether the Australians are ours.</strong> A year apart, one district, one rare surname, and <em>no document joining them</em>. The Oto&ccaron;ac baptisms of the 1860s would settle it in an afternoon.</p><p><strong>2. Ninety loose ends.</strong> 422 records, 90 of them attached to nothing, and 92 people hanging off an A&ndash;Z index that is a sorting artefact rather than a person. <strong>That is a defect in the export, not in the family</strong>, and it is counted and named on its own page. <a href='/zubrinic-roots'>The alphabet nodes &rarr;</a></p><p><strong>3. The godparent column, for every other year.</strong> One reading of it, in 1845, produced a prison keeper. The Oto&ccaron;ac registers give a rank to every godparent and the column has been read for two entries out of hundreds.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  papic: {
    eyebrow: "Senj and Selce",
    dek: "Porters and load-carriers on the Senj waterfront who bought an inn — and, one generation on, a woman supervising the floor of the town's cigarette factory. The other half of the family were merchants at Karlobag.",
    body: [
      ["One change in four documents", "<p>The tree lists Josip &laquo;Joso&raquo; Papi&cacute;'s occupations as <strong><em>trhono&scaron;a, bremenar, gostioni&ccaron;ar</em></strong> &mdash; load-carrier, porter, innkeeper &mdash; as though they were three trades in a row. <strong>Four parish registers give him four words, and only one of them is a change.</strong></p><p>At his own wedding in <strong>1885</strong> he is a <strong><em>bremenar</em></strong>; at his daughter Milka's baptism in <strong>1886</strong>, a <strong><em>trhono&scaron;a</em></strong>; at his daughter Anica's in <strong>1888</strong>, a <strong><em>te&zcaron;ak</em></strong>. Those are not three trades. They are <strong>three words a priest reached for to mean a man who works with his body</strong>: burden-carrier, load-carrier, labourer.</p><p>Then at Milka's wedding in <strong>1907</strong> he is a <strong><em>gostioni&ccaron;ar</em></strong>. <strong>That is the change.</strong> He owns the inn.</p>"],
      ["The document all of it rests on: 3 February 1885", "<p>Senj marriages p. 192, entry 5. <strong>Josip Papi&cacute;</strong>, <em>bremenar</em>, of Senj, aged <strong>27</strong>, bachelor, married <strong>Antonia Perpi&cacute;</strong> of <a href='/prpic'>Krivi Put</a>, resident at Senj, aged <strong>26</strong>, spinster. Banns published; married by the chaplain <strong>Mirko Babi&cacute;</strong>.</p><p>His parents: <strong>Juraj Papa, <em>te&zcaron;ak</em></strong> &mdash; a farm labourer &mdash; and <strong>Matia <em>ro&dstrok;.</em> Pilipi&cacute;</strong>. Hers: <strong>Ivan Perpi&cacute;, <em>seljak</em></strong> &mdash; peasant &mdash; and <strong>Luca <em>ro&dstrok;.</em> Perpi&cacute;</strong>, <strong>which supplies a maiden name the tree did not carry: Antonija's mother was herself a Perpi&cacute;</strong>.</p><p>The witnesses are <strong>Franjo</strong> and <strong>Toma Perpi&cacute;</strong>, both peasants &mdash; <strong>the bride's people, standing up in the groom's town</strong>.</p>"],
      ["The tobacco factory", "<p>Their daughter <a href='/people/milka-lucia-papic'>Milka Lucia Papi&cacute;</a> (1886&ndash;1971) &mdash; <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's mother</a> &mdash; was <strong>supervisor of workers at the cigarette factory</strong> in Senj, an address the tree records as <em class='mono'>Ul. Stjepana Radi&cacute;a 4</em>, &laquo;now a textile factory&raquo;.</p><p>She was baptised at Senj on <strong>19 October 1886</strong>, ten days after her birth, with <strong>Josip Glavi&ccaron;i&cacute; and Ivka Filipovi&cacute;</strong> as godparents &mdash; one of the best-cited entries in this archive. <strong>Two generations: a man carrying sacks on the quay, and his daughter running a factory floor.</strong> <a href='/milka'>Milka &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["The other half of this family, and it is not peasants", "<p>Hedviga's descent is not all labourers, and the other half arrives through this family. <a href='/people/josip-anton-papic'>Josip Papi&cacute;'s</a> mother was <a href='/people/matia-matthea-pilipic'>Matija Pilipi&cacute;</a>, and through her the line runs into <strong>Karlobag</strong>, a free royal city on the coast under the Velebit. <strong>The register there speaks a different language about them.</strong></p><p><strong><em>Dominus</em></strong> at the 1768 baptism of Josephus Pilipi&cacute;. <strong><em>negotiator</em></strong> &mdash; <strong>merchant</strong> &mdash; when he is named as deceased in 1838. And godparents who are <strong>Franciscus de Justiniani</strong>, a Venetian patrician name, and, in 1777, <strong>Pro-Colonel Joannes Hallinich de Zirfeld</strong> with the wife of <strong>Centurion Daniel de Kalbenaeck</strong>.</p><div class='note'>A colonel and a captain's wife stood for an Uroda child; a Giustiniani for a Pilipi&cacute;. <strong>That is not the world of <a href='/blazevic'>Smokvica 114</a></strong> &mdash; and the two meet in one woman four generations later.</div>"],
      ["A name changing in front of us", "<p>The Selce line is entered as <a href='/people/georgius-juraj-joannis-papa-antic-papic'>&laquo;Georgius Juraj (Joannis) <em>Papa</em> &lsquo;Jure&rsquo; <strong>Anti&cacute; Papi&cacute;</strong>&raquo;</a> &mdash; carrying <em>Anti&cacute;</em> and <em>Papi&cacute;</em> at once &mdash; and the tree writes <strong><em>Papi&cacute; (Papa)</em></strong> for a large part of this family. <a href='/name'>/name/ counts them</a>, and counts them again on every build, because this page said <em>seventy-nine</em> for a fortnight after the real figure had moved.</p><p><strong>The register settles that this is a real alternation and not a transcriber's slip.</strong> In the <strong>1885 marriage entry</strong> the groom is <strong>Josip <em>Papi&cacute;</em></strong> and his father, <em>on the very same line</em>, is <strong>Juraj <em>Papa</em></strong>.</p><p><strong>And the doubling is old.</strong> The <strong>1806</strong> baptismal entry for <a href='/people/josephum-antic'>Josephum</a> at Vinodol enters his mother as <em>&laquo;matre Marthe <strong>Antich Papa</strong>&raquo;</em> &mdash; both names on one line, eighty years before the tree's <em>Papi&cacute; (Papa)</em>. His godfather is the village <strong>judge</strong>, <em>Judice Mathia Jellichich</em> &mdash; of the family he would marry into.</p>"],
      ["Four deaths read in October 2026, and what each did to the tree", "<p>The Selce and Karlobag death registers were opened for the direct ancestors still without a document of their own. <strong>Selce:</strong> <a href='/people/anna-papic'>Anna Papi&cacute;</a> died <strong>24 August 1807</strong> aged about 65, entered <em>&laquo;uxor Pauli Lon&ccaron;arich Papich&raquo;</em> at house <strong>134</strong> &mdash; her own surname on a document for the first time &mdash; and <a href='/people/pauli-loncaric'>Pauli Lon&ccaron;ari&cacute;</a> died <strong>29 September 1811</strong> a widower, aged about 70, <strong>at house 134 again</strong>. Both dates and both ages are the tree's. <strong>Karlobag:</strong> <a href='/people/josephus-josip-pilipic'>Josephus Pilipi&cacute;</a> is entered <em>&laquo;Josephus Pilepich Carlobagensis, annorum 61&raquo;</em> and the register gives <strong>28 June 1828</strong> where the tree has 26 &mdash; a probable match, not a proven one &mdash; and <a href='/people/lucia-maja-tomljanovic'>Lucia &laquo;Maja&raquo;</a> is <em>&laquo;uxor Michaelis Gergacs&raquo;</em> on <strong>19 September 1809</strong>, the tree's day and by-name and husband, <strong>but at an age of 34 that cannot be the mother of a child baptised in 1789</strong>.</p><p><strong>And one name is wrong.</strong> On <strong>27 February 1793</strong> a man dies at Selce aged about 54 &mdash; the day, month and age of the tree's <a href='/people/joannis-antic-2'>Joannis Anti&cacute;</a> &mdash; but the register calls him <strong>Antonius</strong> Antich <em>Martini</em>. Either the tree has the right man under the wrong first name, or this is a different Antich who died the same day; this page does not choose, and the entry is not counted as a reading of ahnentafel 96. The detail is in <a href='/corrections'>the corrections</a> and <a href='/readings'>the readings</a>.</p>"],
      ["Why the Papići have no house number", "<p>The <a href='/blazevic'>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;i</a> can be traced through a single house number because the <strong>village</strong> registers of Krmpote and Krivi Put record every child against one &mdash; <em>Smokvica 114</em>, <em>Krivi Put 136</em>. <strong>The Senj register does not.</strong> Under <em>mjesto obitavanja i ku&cacute;ni broj</em> &mdash; place of residence and house number &mdash; the Papi&cacute; entries say simply <strong>&laquo;Senj&raquo;</strong>, and nothing else.</p><p>That is a difference between a town and a village, not between two families. <strong>It also means the <a href='/blazevic'>zadruga</a> that is visible at Smokvica would be invisible here even if it existed</strong>, and no amount of reading Senj baptisms will show it.</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Whether Anti&cacute; and Papi&cacute; were ever two families.</strong> The alternation is proved back to 1806 and unexplained. The Vinodol and Selce registers before 1806 would say.</p><p><strong>2. The marriage of Juraj Papa and Matija Pilipi&cacute;.</strong> Their son was baptised 1 November 1857, so the marriage falls just before it; it is not in any index this archive has probed. <strong>It is not on the Karlobag or Selce marriage pages for 1856 and 1857</strong> (read in October 2026), and the Senj marriage book for those years is not in date order, so it can only be found by walking it; it would add a date and witnesses and no new parents. It is <a href='/worklist'>row 4</a>, parked.</p><p><strong>3. What happened to the inn.</strong> Josip is a <em>gostioni&ccaron;ar</em> in 1907 and nothing after that says where, or whether it passed to anyone.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  prpic: {
    eyebrow: "Krivi Put",
    dek: "Thirty-five people born at one house number over four generations — and the document that proved Krivi Put was a Military Frontier village, found by re-reading a scan filed as a duplicate.",
    body: [
      ["Thirty-five people at one house number", "<p><strong>Thirty-five people</strong> in this tree are born at <strong class='mono'>Krivi Put 136</strong>. House numbers are how Frontier registers identify a family, and a settled household can hold one for a century.</p><blockquote class='note'><strong>Krivi Put, entry 51 of 1859</strong> &mdash; kept in Latin<br /><em>nata</em> <strong>12 May 1859</strong>, <em>baptisata</em> <strong>15 May</strong> &middot; <strong><em>Antonia Perpi&cacute;, legit.</em></strong><br />daughter of the spouses <strong>Joannes and Lucia Perpi&cacute;</strong> &middot; <em>locus domicilii</em> <strong>Krivi Put N&deg; 136</strong><br /><span class='dek'>godparents <strong>Lucas Tomljanovi&cacute;</strong> and <strong>Anna Tomljanovi&cacute;, daughter of Andreas</strong></span></blockquote><p><a href='/people/antonija-tonka-perpic-prpic'>Antonija</a> is the woman the whole page hangs from: she married into <a href='/papic'>the Papi&cacute;i</a> of Senj in 1885, and she is <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's</a> grandmother.</p>"],
      ["They were Frontier households too, and nobody had noticed", "<p>The fuller of the two scans of that 1859 baptism carries a <em>conditio</em> column the other crop omits, and the word in it is <strong><em>Confiniar.</em></strong> &mdash; <strong><em>confiniarii</em></strong>, men of the Military Frontier.</p><p><strong>Nor is it one household.</strong> On the same opening the <strong>Perpi&cacute;</strong>, <strong>Tomljanovi&cacute;</strong> and <strong>Butkovi&cacute;</strong> families are all entered the same way.</p><div class='note'><strong>So Krivi Put was a Frontier village, exactly as <a href='/frontier'>Oto&ccaron;ac</a> was.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>This archive held the <a href='/bunjevci'>Bunjevac settlement</a> and the <a href='/zubrinic'>&Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; <em>confiniarii</em></a> as two separate facts for weeks and never joined them. <strong>It came out of re-reading a document that had been filed as a duplicate.</strong></span></div>"],
      ["And the page proves the village", "<p>Read the other entries on the same opening and the <a href='/bunjevci'>Bunjevac claim</a> stops being an inference from place-names and becomes an observation. The surnames baptised at Krivi Put in the summer of 1859 are <strong>Perpi&cacute;, Tomljanovi&cacute;, Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;, &Scaron;ojat, Paveli&cacute;, Vukeli&cacute;, Butkovi&cacute;</strong> &mdash; and <strong>Krmpoti&cacute;</strong>, the clan the whole settlement is named for.</p><p>Every child is entered against a house number: N&deg; 4, 14, 15, 18, 23, 41, 51, 54, 58, 61, 72, 79, 81, 82, <strong>136</strong>. <strong>Three of this archive's own surnames are on one summer's page of one mountain parish.</strong></p>"],
      ["Prpić or Perpić — a decision, written down", "<p>The tree writes it <strong>Prpic 83 times, Perpic 19, Perpi&cacute; 16, Prpi&cacute; 8</strong>, once as <em>Perpich</em>, and once &mdash; with admirable honesty &mdash; as <em class='mono'>&laquo;Papi&cacute; or Perpic&raquo;</em>. Antonija is entered under <strong>both</strong>: &laquo;Perpi&cacute; / Prpic&raquo;.</p><p><strong>This archive treats Prpi&cacute; and Perpi&cacute; as one name.</strong> That is a decision rather than a finding, and it is recorded as one, here and in the code that folds them. <a href='/name'>The names &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Why Antonija is at Mirogoj.</strong> She died at Senj on <strong>2 February 1935</strong> and is buried at <strong>Mirogoj in Zagreb</strong> &mdash; the monumental cemetery, a long way from Krivi Put and a long way from Senj. <strong>Nothing in the record explains it</strong>, and it is the kind of thing a family knows and a register does not. <a href='/open-questions'>Open questions &rarr;</a></p><p><strong>2. Who held Krivi Put 136 before 1820.</strong> The house is the spine of this family and this archive has it for four generations. The Krivi Put baptisms run from 1847; <strong>anything earlier is at Senj or at Krmpote</strong>, and neither has been walked for this surname.</p><p><strong>3. Whether Lucia Perpi&cacute;, Antonija's mother, is kin to her husband.</strong> The 1885 marriage gives her maiden name as <strong>Perpi&cacute; too</strong>. In a village of fifteen house numbers that is likely and unproved.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  kalanj: {
    eyebrow: "Brinje, then the coast",
    dek: "This archive looked for them at Klenovica for a fortnight. Their own baptismal entry calls them country people of the parish of Brinje, forty kilometres inland — and not one page of that register has been opened.",
    body: [
      ["They are not a Klenovica family. They are Brinje people.", "<p>This archive spent a fortnight treating the Kalanji as a <strong>Klenovica</strong> family. <strong>119 of them are recorded there</strong>, eighty-five carry a Klenovica or Povile house number, and every search this archive has run has been on that coast. <strong>Their own baptismal entry says otherwise</strong>, and it took an American passenger manifest to make anyone read the line properly.</p><blockquote class='note'><strong>Klenovica N&deg; 22, 3 January 1864</strong> &mdash; the baptism of <strong>Ana Kalanj</strong><br /><em>Josephus Kalanj et Rosalia nata Perkovi&cacute; ejus uxor,</em> <strong><em>Brinjensis parochiae ruricolae</em></strong><br /><span class='dek'>&mdash; country people of the parish of <strong>Brinje</strong>.</span></blockquote><p>Brinje is <em>inland</em>, over the Kapela, some forty kilometres from the sea. And the tree independently records <strong>Rozalija Perkovi&cacute;</strong> as being of Brinje. So the Klenovica Kalanji came <em>down</em> to the coast, and this page is written the wrong way round: everything below is what the family looked like <strong>after</strong> they arrived.</p><div class='note'><strong>Brinje does not appear in the <a href='/gazetteer'>gazetteer</a>, and not one page of its register has been opened.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>That is the single largest hole in this family, and it is a hole this archive dug itself by believing a house number over a parish name. <a href='/searched'>What has been searched &rarr;</a></span></div>"],
      ["And three of our surnames meet at Brinje", "<p>The emigration records closed the triangle, and they are the reason the Latin line was read again at all. <strong>Stipe Kalanj</strong>, born 1885, sailed for New York in <strong>1910</strong> on <em>La Bretagne</em>, gave his residence as <strong>Brinje</strong>, left a wife Kate behind, and was met at the dock by his <strong>cousin, Juro Bori&cacute;</strong>.</p><p>This tree holds a <strong>Jure Bori&cacute; <em>of Brinje</em></strong> &mdash; whose daughter <strong>Ana Bori&cacute;</strong>, born 12 February 1885 at Brinje, married <strong>Frane Papi&cacute;</strong> of <a href='/senj'>Senj</a>, born 27 July 1873. <a href='/papic'>Papi&cacute;</a> is <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's mother's</a> family.</p><p><strong>Kalanj, Perkovi&cacute; and Bori&cacute; are all at Brinje, and a Bori&cacute; of Brinje married a Papi&cacute; of Senj.</strong> That is not proof they are one kin-group and it is not offered as one. It is the observation that <strong>the parish where three of this archive's nine surnames overlap is the one parish nobody has ever looked in</strong>.</p>"],
      ["What the coast records do show: one house under two names", "<p>Once they are on the coast the Kalanji are the best-placed family in this archive &mdash; <strong>87% of their records name a place</strong> &mdash; and the places resolve into far fewer houses than they first appear to.</p><p>Eighty-five published Kalanji carry a Klenovica or Povile house number, and <strong>every one of those houses is entirely Kalanj</strong> &mdash; not one is shared with another surname. The two largest looked separate and are not. Petrus Kalanj and Magdalena Butkovi&cacute;'s ten children are entered <em>Povile 22, Klenovica 22, Klenovica 22, Povile 22</em>, then four at Klenovica 22, then two more at Povile 22. Ivan and Josipa alternate the same way across seven children; Luka and Kata across four.</p><p><strong>No household moves house nine times and alternates back.</strong> Povile and Klenovica are neighbouring settlements and the registers used the two names for one place. So <strong>house 22 holds 67 published people between 1840 and 1890</strong> &mdash; the largest single household in the archive, ahead of <a href='/blazevic'>Smokvica 114</a> and <a href='/prpic'>Krivi Put 136</a>. <a href='/gazetteer'>The gazetteer &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["And the smaller numbers are zadrugas too", "<p>If house 22 is the extended household, the small late numbers ought to be sons splitting off. Two of them are not. <strong>Klenovica 23</strong> (1862&ndash;1893) holds the children of <strong>four</strong> different couples &mdash; Mate and Catharina Pa&hellip;, Josephus and Catharina Buneta, Jandre and Manda Komadina, Joso and Manda Komadina. <strong>Klenovica 36</strong> (1892&ndash;1894) holds the children of <strong>three</strong> &mdash; Luka and Kata Mileti&cacute;, Joso and Manda, Franjo and Tereza.</p><p>That is the same signature this archive identified at <a href='/blazevic'>Smokvica 114</a>: several married couples entered against one number in the same years. So the <strong><em>zadruga</em></strong> is not a Smokvica peculiarity or a Bunjevac one &mdash; <strong>it is how Klenovica was organised too</strong>, and a family that had just come down from Brinje kept it. Houses 37, 38 and 59 hold a single child each and are something else, probably transient.</p><h3>One puzzle sits inside it</h3><p><strong>Luka Kalanj and Kata Mileti&cacute;</strong> have children at house 22, then at 36 in 1892, then at 37 in 1894 &mdash; and two of them are called <strong>Marija</strong>. Whether that is a family moving twice in two years, a renumbering, or two entries for one child <strong>is not answerable from this file</strong>.</p>"],
      ["Four boys called Antonius", "<p>Petrus and Magdalena's children include an <em>Antonius</em> born in 1865, another in 1868, a third in 1868 and a fourth in 1872. <strong>That is not an error.</strong> It is a <strong>necronym</strong> &mdash; a dead child's name given to the next born &mdash; and it is why the <a href='/duplicates'>duplicate check</a> has to read two death years as proof of two people rather than as evidence of one.</p>"],
      ["How they reach Hedviga", "<p>Not, as this page used to say, by marrying into the Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; line &mdash; that is one marriage, Lovro Kalanj and Klara Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;. The chain that actually carries the family runs through <strong>Hedviga's husband</strong>.</p><p><strong>Ana Kalanj</strong>, born at <strong>Klenovica N&deg; 22 on 3 January 1864</strong> and baptised three days later &mdash; the same entry that says Brinje &mdash; married <strong>Josip de Franceschi</strong>; their son Ante, their grandson Pepe. Every published Kalanj is connected to Hedviga by ordinary links: <strong>100%</strong>, which only one other family here can say. <a href='/buckets'>What that means &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["Four siblings, found loose in a photo library", "<p>The baptisms of <strong>Ana (1894), Franciska (1896), Zora (1898) and Ivan (1900)</strong> &mdash; four consecutive children of Martin Kalanj and Marija Peri&ccaron;i&cacute; at Novi Vinodolski &mdash; were sitting unattached in the family's own photographs. All four match the tree <strong>to the day</strong>, and the register calls the father <em>Martin Kalanj <strong>Josipov</strong></em>, a patronymic that confirms the grandfather independently of the tree.</p><p>Zora's godparents in 1898 are entered <em>Josip i Ana Defrancesco</em> &mdash; the child's <strong>aunt and uncle</strong>, which is how Ana's own place in this family was confirmed <strong>from a direction nobody was looking in</strong>.</p>"],
      ["A death this page was too credulous about", "<div class='fix'><h3>Corrected the same day it was published</h3><p>This page said, for most of a day, that <strong>Martin Kalanj died at Trebinje on 5 August 1900</strong> &mdash; in Herzegovina, four hundred kilometres from home, seven months after his youngest child was baptised at Novi Vinodolski &mdash; and that nothing here explained it. <strong>That was too credulous.</strong></p><p>His <em>birth</em> carries a full citation: FamilySearch <span class='mono'>3QSQ-G99X-KW8J</span>, image 17 of 342, Hrvatski dr&zcaron;avni arhiv image group 005494765. His <em>death</em> carries <strong>none</strong> &mdash; the source is a family tree, and the place is the bare word <em>Trebinje</em> with no country attached.</p><p>And this tree moves Lika villages into Bosnia. <strong>Michael &laquo;Mate&raquo; &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;, born 1863 at Prozor near Oto&ccaron;ac, is geocoded in the same export to Prozor in the Herzegovina-Neretva Canton</strong> &mdash; two hundred kilometres away, across a border, and the <a href='/places'>fourth geocoding error</a> recorded here.</p><p><strong>So the question is no longer why he went to Herzegovina. It is whether he ever did</strong> &mdash; and a death register at Novi Vinodolski or Klenovica would answer it faster than any search in Trebinje.</p></div><div class='note'><strong>The superseded text, kept.</strong> <em>&laquo;Martin Kalanj died at Trebinje on 5 August 1900, in Herzegovina, four hundred kilometres from home &mdash; seven months after his youngest child was baptised at Novi Vinodolski. He was thirty-eight, with five children under seven. Military service or work following the garrison are the obvious readings and this archive has evidence for neither.&raquo;</em> <a href='/changed'>What changed &rarr;</a></div>"],
      ["Brinje, opened at last — and a Kalanj was there the whole time", "<p>Asked for directly on 29 September 2026. DGS 005498262, Brinje's own <em>Vjen&#269;ani</em> (marriages) volume, read from its first page for the first time since this row was opened on 15 September. <strong>Image 530</strong> holds two entries facing each other, and both bear on the questions below.</p><blockquote class='note'><strong>Brinje, 16 [month illegible] 1890</strong> &mdash; entry no. 13<br /><em>KALANJ NIKO, bachelor, 40, of Brinje house 231, son of KALANJ JURO and ANA</em>, marries a widow recorded as Marija, 33, of Brinje house 198. Witnesses Zori&#269;i&#263; Stjepan and Rimenovi&#263; Tomo.</blockquote><p><strong>Niko Kalanj, born about 1850, and his father Juro Kalanj</strong> are the first Kalanj men this archive has found actually resident at Brinje itself &mdash; not a coast family remembered as being &laquo;of&raquo; the parish on the strength of one Latin phrase, but a household in it, at a numbered house, a generation before anyone here can otherwise reach. Neither is in the tree; both are recorded at <a href='/found/niko-kalanj/'>/found/niko-kalanj/</a>. <strong>That marriage is now a confirmed household</strong>: ANICA KALANJ, daughter of this same Niko Kalanj and a mother named Marija Vu&#269;eti&#263;, was born at the same Brinje house 231 on 21 August 1893 &mdash; the mother's maiden name fits cleanly, with none of the contradictions the Bori&#263; puzzles below carry (<a href='/found/anica-kalanj/'>/found/anica-kalanj/</a>). <strong>A second child followed three years later</strong>: a son, also named NIKO after his own father, born 26 July 1896 - the same two parents, the same clean line, no discrepancy anywhere in either reading.</p><blockquote class='note'><strong>The facing page, same image</strong> &mdash; entry no. 13<br /><em>BORI&#262; ___, recorded here as «Vid» though the given name resists a confident reading</em>, widower(?), 25, of Kamenica (a hamlet of Brinje parish) house 17, son of BORI&#262; LUKA, marries a woman recorded as (&#352;)ori&#263; Kate, 20, of Brinje house 127. A second witness on the same entry is BORI&#262; JOZO.</blockquote><p><strong>Three Bori&#263; men, independent of the emigration papers that first named one.</strong> This page has said since it was written that a Jure Bori&#263; of Brinje gave away his daughter to a Papi&#263; of Senj &mdash; sourced from a 1910 passenger manifest, not from any Croatian register. This is the first confirmation from the parish's own book that Bori&#263; is a real, resident Kamenica surname, with Luka, this groom, and Jozo all attested in 1890. Whether any of the three is kin to the Jure Bori&#263; already on this page is <strong>not established</strong> &mdash; recorded at <a href='/found/vid-boric/'>/found/vid-boric/</a>.</p><div class='note'><strong>The whole marriage book has since been read, image 493 to its own «KRAJ» card at image 690.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>It holds three Kalanj men (Niko, his father Juro, and a second household under Mijo, with a fourth, Mate, standing witness) and nine Bori&#263; men and women across four Brinje-area houses, and three Perkovi&#263; — the surname the Gospi&#263;'s letter specifically asked this archive to find at Brinje, finally attested as a resident family rather than a name in a letter. Thirteen people, none of them in the tree, all with their own <code>/found/</code> page. <strong>The first Ro&#273;eni (births) item is now finished too</strong> &mdash; it turned out to be catalogued 1888&ndash;1892, a shorter and separate item from the marriage book rather than one long 492-image volume as first assumed. It added four more Bori&#263; and two Kalanj women to the tally &mdash; including a second Bori&#263;-marries-Bori&#263; household, at Brinje house 191, distinct from the one this book's marriages already showed at house 141 &mdash; and a likely third child at that very house. A second Ro&#273;eni item, 1893&ndash;1897, is open now on the same film. <a href='/worklist'>The work list &rarr;</a></span></div><p><strong>That second item, read to image 218 of its own pages so far, has already answered one question this page asked and opened two more.</strong> <strong>VIOL KALANJ, of Brinje, married to Kate Zori&#269;i&#263; since a birth in 1890, now has a second child on record</strong>: FRANJO KALANJ, born 10 August 1893 &mdash; proof this is a real, continuing household and not a single register entry, at <a href='/found/franjo-kalanj/'>/found/franjo-kalanj/</a>. The house number given for Franjo, 189, does not match the 118 recorded for his sister three years earlier; not explained.</p><p><strong>Two more open puzzles, both about whether a household already on this page has grown a second name for itself.</strong> At Kamenica house 24 &mdash; already the address of a Niko Bori&#263; whose daughter Marija married out in 1897 &mdash; a second daughter, KATE BORI&#262;, was born in February 1893, but her mother is recorded as Radoti&#263; Dace, not the Bare named for Marija (<a href='/found/kate-boric/'>/found/kate-boric/</a>); and then a third birth at the very same house, LUCE BORI&#262;, born October 1893 to a father entered as Tome, not Niko, and a mother Marija Radoti&#263;, not Dace (<a href='/found/luce-boric-daughter-of-tome-boric/'>/found/luce-boric-daughter-of-tome-boric/</a>). Three children at one house number, two fathers' names, two mothers' names: a second wife, an alternate name, a scribal slip, or genuinely more than one Bori&#263; household sharing the number &mdash; none of it established. At Brinje house 126 &mdash; already the house of Ivan Bori&#263;'s children Juko and Luce &mdash; a son, STJEPAN BORI&#262;, was born in August 1893 to a father named Grgo, not Ivan (<a href='/found/stjepan-boric-son-of-grgo-boric/'>/found/stjepan-boric-son-of-grgo-boric/</a>). Both read as written, not merged into the household already known. <strong>That Grgo Bori&#263; household is now confirmed rather than a single register line</strong>: a second child, ANA BORI&#262;, was born to the same Grgo Bori&#263; and Marija Milakovi&#263; at the same house on Christmas Day 1894 (<a href='/found/ana-boric-daughter-of-grgo-boric/'>/found/ana-boric-daughter-of-grgo-boric/</a>) &mdash; a real, continuing family, though whether Grgo and Ivan/Ive are the same man is still not established.</p><p><strong>And one puzzle this page has carried since the marriage book was read is now a birth, not just a marriage entry.</strong> That book records a MARIJA BORI&#262;, spinster, 19, of Brinje house 142, daughter of Niko Bori&#263;, marrying HOFJENC NIKO around 1892&ndash;93. A son, also named NIKO, was born to a Niko Hofjenc and Marija Bori&#263; on 11 October 1893 &mdash; almost exactly the timeline that marriage implies, and the first direct proof it actually took place and produced a family, not only a register line (<a href='/found/niko-hofjenc-son-of-niko-hofjenc-and-marija-boric/'>/found/niko-hofjenc-son-of-niko-hofjenc-and-marija-boric/</a>). One thing left unexplained: a witness on the same entry is entered under the mother's own name, Bori&#263; Marija.</p><p><strong>Images 230 to 239, the most recent stretch, add a third address to the Kamenica puzzle and a plausible face for the Grgo Bori&#263; named above.</strong> A Horman&#263; household now also sits at Kamenica house 24, alongside the Bori&#263; and Serti&#263; households already recorded there &mdash; a third surname sharing one number, favouring the shared-address reading over one confused family, though still not proof either way. And a GRGO BORI&#262; stood witness at a Kamenica baptism the same year as Stjepan's birth above &mdash; the same given name, same parish, four months after the father named for Stjepan, but no document names them as one man; kept as a plausible, unconfirmed match, not a merge.</p><p><strong>And the register turned over into 1894 to add a fourth child to the one household on this page with no puzzle left in it.</strong> BORI&#262; OVE, born 10 January 1894 at Brinje house 141, daughter of BORI&#262; TOME and BORI&#262; JELE &mdash; a fourth child, joining JURAJ (1888), PETAR (1890) and MATIJA (1892), and the first of the four with no discrepancy in the mother's name to puzzle over. Four children, one house, one clean line: still the steadiest Bori&#263; household this page has found &mdash; until March 1895, when the same house number produced BORI&#262; MARICA, daughter of BORI&#262; IVE and BORI&#262; MATIJA, not Tome and Jela. The 1892 birth already carried an uncertain reading of the mother's name as Matija; whether this is a second Bori&#263;-marries-Bori&#263; household sharing house 141, or the same misreading recurring, is not established.</p><p><strong>A fifth Kamenica house number for the surname, and a witness whose own name resists a confident reading.</strong> BORI&#262; LUCE, born 6 February 1894 at Kamenica house 83, daughter of BORI&#262; LOJZE and Mesi&#263; Manda-Marija &mdash; a new Bori&#263; given name at a new address, kept apart from the already-puzzling house 24 and from the three other women named Luce Bori&#263; already on this page. That exact mother's name resurfaced four months later at a different Kamenica house: BORI&#262; TOME, born June 1895 at house 23, son of BORI&#262; TOME and the same Mesi&#263; Manda-Marija &mdash; a different father's name and a different house number, so not merged, but the coincidence is flagged rather than ignored. Lojze himself may have resurfaced under his full name: BORI&#262; ALOJZ stood witness to a &#352;korup baptism at Brinje, alongside the same &#352;korup surname his own household's witnesses carried at Luce's baptism &mdash; a stronger coincidence than most of this page's unconfirmed links, though still not a named match. A new given name reached Kamenica in August 1895, when BORI&#262; STJEPAN stood witness to a Serti&#263; baptism there. A ninth BORI&#262; MARIJA stood witness at another Kamenica baptism the same year, kept apart from all the others of that name already here. And a Bori&#263; man witnessed a second Milakovi&#263; baptism &mdash; the same pairing this page already noted once, a Bori&#263; standing up for a Milakovi&#263; child, though nothing ties the two sightings to one man. <strong>A third sighting followed, at the very same house.</strong> BORI&#262; GRGO stood witness to a Milakovi&#263; baptism at Brinje house 74 in April 1897 &mdash; the identical house where he first appeared in this file in 1893, paired again with a woman named Marica. The strongest recurring pattern this page has found, though still short of a confirmed identity. A new given name, BORI&#262; VIOL, survives only as a witness line at the very top of a page whose own entry was never fully photographed. Another new given name, BORI&#262; ROZA, stood witness at a Brinje baptism the same September. And a third Jozo/Joso Bori&#263; turned up witnessing a &#352;korup baptism &mdash; the same surname already tied to the plausible Alojz Bori&#263; sighting above, though this one is kept separate.</p><p> And BORI&#262; TRICA stood witness at a Brinje baptism in April 1894 &mdash; a given name this page is not confident it has read correctly, and nothing more than a witness line to go on.</p><p><strong>And, at the very edge of this month's reading, a name that may close the loop this page opened first.</strong> BORI&#262; VID stood witness to a baptism at Brinje house 132 on 30 May 1894 &mdash; a plausible, unconfirmed match for the first Bori&#263; this page ever named, back in the 1890 marriage book (son of Luka Bori&#263;, Kamenica house 17), though that earlier reading of his own name was itself never a confident one. And a sixth woman named BORI&#262; MARIJA married out of the surname, this time into Krnari&#263; &mdash; kept apart from the five others of the same name already here, since nothing ties any of them together beyond the name itself.</p><p><strong>A second unmarried Bori&#263; mother, at a Kamenica house number this page has not seen before.</strong> KATE BORI&#262;, of Kamenica house 3, had a daughter in July 1894 who was entered under her mother's own surname, OVE BORI&#262; &mdash; the father's column left blank, the same pattern this page already carries once, at a different Kamenica house, for Luce Bori&#263; and her son Marko in 1891. The two women are kept apart; the pattern itself is now the only thing they share. The same baptism names a witness, a seventh BORI&#262; MARIJA in this file &mdash; possibly a relative standing up for the child, though nothing says so. And a fifth woman named BORI&#262; LUCE married out of the surname, into Bi&#353;ani&#263; &mdash; an eighth Bori&#263; woman married out of this parish's most persistently exported name. <strong>That marriage is now a confirmed household too</strong>: a second son, IVAN, was born to the same Marko Bi&#353;ani&#263; and Luce Bori&#263; at the same house 174 two years later, in September 1896.</p><p><strong>A third Niko Bori&#263; household, at a house number this page has not carried before.</strong> BORI&#262; MARKO was born 19 September 1894 at Brinje house 148, son of BORI&#262; NIKO and Boleti&#263; Marija &mdash; a different Niko Bori&#263; from both the one who married in 1890 at house 142 and the one already on this page as father of Marija, Kate and Luce at Kamenica house 24. Three men, three houses, one given name: the same shape this page keeps finding.</p><p><strong>And a plausible return, three years on.</strong> BORI&#262; MANOLE &mdash; already on this page as a mother at Kamenica house 24 by 1891 &mdash; stood witness to a Kamenica baptism in September 1894, probably the same woman, though nothing names her as such. <strong>The register then turned into 1895</strong>, and a first Bori&#263; name reached Lu&#269;ane: BORI&#262; IVAN stood witness there on 27 December 1894, the same hamlet a Bori&#263; woman had already married into in 1893.</p><p><strong>An eighth Marija Bori&#263; married out, into the same family a Marija Bori&#263; had already married into once before.</strong> BORI&#262; MARIJA became the wife of Vid Hofjevac, at Brinje house 160 &mdash; a different husband and a different spelling of the name (Hofjevac, not Hofjenc), but the same family this page's very first Marija Bori&#263; married into at house 142. A witness on the same entry, BORI&#262; MANULE, may or may not be the Manole Bori&#263; already followed at Kamenica; this entry places her at Brinje itself.</p><p><strong>And, for the first time in this register, a Perkovi&#263; woman</strong> &mdash; the surname this whole search began by chasing, alongside Kalanj and Bori&#263;. PERKOVI&#262; KATE became the mother of Hofjevac Luka at Brinje house 148 in August 1896, married into the very same Hofjenc/Hofjevac family that has already taken in several Bori&#263; brides. A fourth BORI&#262; KATE married into Domi&#263; at Lu&#269;ane the following month, kept apart from the three others of that name already here. <strong>And the Serti&#263; household at Kamenica house 13 is now confirmed too</strong>: a second son, NIKO, was born to the same Jure Serti&#263; and Tauka Bori&#263; in November 1896, three and a half years after their first. <strong>The register then turned into 1897</strong>, and the Krnari&#263;-Bori&#263; household this page found first in 1894 grew too: a second child, TOME, born to Jure Krnari&#263; and Marija Bori&#263; on 18 March 1897, this time with a house number (208) the first entry never gave.</p><div class='note'><strong>THIS ITEM IS NOW FINISHED &mdash; image 400 is the film's own «ITEM 2» end card, not a stopping point this archive chose.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>One volume, 1893 to 1897, produced dozens of Bori&#263; individuals across at least a dozen Brinje-area houses, the first Perkovi&#263; woman this parish has attested, and two more children confirming the very first Kalanj household this archive found. None of them are in the tree. Near-miss surnames kept surfacing &mdash; Kalauj, Perhoni&#263;, Perki&#263;, &#272;ori&#263;, Hori&#263;, Verkovi&#263; &mdash; and were first kept distinct rather than folded in for a bigger number. <strong>That was wrong for two of them (Kalauj and Perhoni&#263;); the notes at the end of this entry say why.</strong> <strong>A third item opens right after, on the same film, its entries continuing from no. 143 rather than resetting to 1</strong> &mdash; this book almost certainly runs past 1897 without a fresh title card announcing it. Not yet opened. <a href='/worklist'>The work list &rarr;</a></span></div><p><strong>That third item is now confirmed to be exactly that</strong> &mdash; the same register continuing past 1897 with no fresh title card of its own, entry numbers running straight on from where item two left off. A new BORI&#262; NIKO stood witness to a Bla&#382;anin baptism at Brinje house 154 in December 1897, kept separate from the Niko Bori&#263; already on this page.</p><p><strong>And the register turned into calendar year 1898, adding a probable third child to the very first Niko Bori&#263; household this page named.</strong> BORI&#262; MARICA was born 23 July 1898 at Brinje house 142, daughter of BORI&#262; NIKO and a mother's maiden name entered as Bojeni&#263; &mdash; a third spelling for that same wife, after Ba&#353;i&#263; in the 1890 marriage record and Bo&#353;nji&#263; for their first child Viol in 1891. Close enough in sound that all three may be one woman under three scribal hands, though this page does not merge them on sound alone. A witness on the same entry, BORI&#262; TOME, is kept separate from the two other Tome Bori&#263; men already here &mdash; one a lone witness at house 101 in 1893, the other heading his own household at Kamenica house 23.</p><p><strong>A second Serti&#263;-Bori&#263; couple, at a Kamenica house number the surname has not carried before.</strong> SERTI&#262; NIKO was born 10 October 1898 at Kamenica house 28, son of Serti&#263; Jure and BORI&#262; FRANCE &mdash; kept apart from the Serti&#263; Jure and Tauka Bori&#263; already confirmed on this page at Kamenica house 13, since the house number and the wife's given name both differ, though both husbands share the name Jure. A witness on the same entry, BORI&#262; VUJO, is a new given name for the surname. Two more names brushed past without being pulled in: a mother recorded as BORI&#262;EVI&#262; MARIJA, a different surname sharing only the root, watched rather than counted; and a second JURE KRNARI&#262;, married to Dra&#382;enovi&#263; Ova at Brinje house 24, kept separate from the Krnari&#263;-Bori&#263; household already on this page at house 208.</p><p><strong>And the register turned into 1899, confirming a name this page had only ever named in passing.</strong> BORI&#262; MANOLE was born 3 January 1899 at Kamenica house 23, daughter of BORI&#262; ALOJZ and Mesi&#263; Manola &mdash; the same house number already holding Tome Bori&#263; (born 1895, son of Bori&#263; Tome and Mesi&#263; Manda-Marija), and the same wife's surname, Mesi&#263;, for a second time at that address. Alojz Bori&#263; himself was on this page only as a single witness sighting, tentatively linked to Lojze Bori&#263; of Kamenica house 83 (also married to a Mesi&#263; woman); this baptism proves he is a real household head, though it does not say whether he is Tome, Lojze, both, or neither. Three Bori&#263; men, two houses, one recurring wife's surname &mdash; still not merged.</p><p><strong>A new Kalanj name, at the very house this whole page began with.</strong> KALANJ RU&#381;E was born 5 January 1899 at Brinje house 231, daughter of Kalanj Jure and Vu&#269;eti&#263; Manole &mdash; the same house already carrying Niko Kalanj's household (married 1890, son of Kalanj Juro and Ana). <em>Corrected on re-reading at full zoom: the mother's surname was first entered here as Vukeli&#263;; it is Vu&#269;eti&#263;, the same surname as Niko's own wife, Marija Vu&#269;eti&#263;.</em> Jure is a variant of Juro, so this is probably the same family, but she is kept as a separate entry, not folded into it.</p><p><strong>Reading resumed, and the right-hand page of that same spread had been skipped.</strong> On image 450 itself, BORI&#262; LUCE was born 19 January 1899 at Brinje house 141, daughter of Bori&#263; Tome and Bori&#263; Jela &mdash; a fifth child for the Bori&#263;-marries-Bori&#263; household (Juraj 1888, Petar 1890, Matija 1892, Ove 1894), with a BORI&#262; NIKO as witness. On the next image, KALANJ FRANC was born 28 January 1899 at <strong>house 231 again</strong>, son of Kalanj <em>Mile</em> and Vu&#269;eti&#263; Marija. Two Kalanj births at the same house within four weeks, both to Vu&#269;eti&#263; mothers, cannot be one child: either Niko's household is being entered under shifting first names, or two Kalanj brothers shared the house and married into the same Vu&#269;eti&#263; family. Neither is established, and neither entry is merged into Niko's.</p><p><strong>Two households this page already knew each gained a third child in March 1899.</strong> KRNARI&#262; MIJO was born 20 March at Brinje house 208, son of Jure Krnari&#263; and Bori&#263; Marija &mdash; after Manda (1894) and Tome (1897), at the same house; the second Krnari&#263; household entered at house 208 in November 1898 now looks like a second family sharing the address, not a rival reading of this one. And KALANJ MARIJA was born 13 March at Brinje house 188, a third child for Viol Kalanj and Kate Zori&#269;i&#263; (Kate 1890, Franjo 1893): the house number now reads 118, 189 and 188 across the three births, which looks like a habit of the register, not three addresses. A witness on one entry reads Perhovi&#263;, the near-miss, not Perkovi&#263;.</p><p><strong>August 1899 is the densest run of Bori&#263; names this page has met since 1895, and it points the same way as everything before it: Serti&#263;.</strong> BORI&#262; MARIJA's second child with Hofjevac Viol (Vid, in the 1895 entry) was born 9 August at house 160 &mdash; the same woman, not a ninth. Two days earlier, SERTI&#262; TOMO was born at house 128 to Serti&#263; Petar and <strong>BORI&#262; KATE</strong>, with a BORI&#262; JELA as witness; and six days later SERTI&#262; RU&#381;E was born at the neighbouring house 127 to Serti&#263; <em>Marko</em> and <strong>BORI&#262; KATE</strong>. Two husbands, two houses, one mother's name and surname inside a week: two women both called Kate Bori&#263; who married into Serti&#263;, or one woman and a clerk's slip. Not resolved. At Kamenica, BORI&#262; LOJZE stood witness to a Serti&#263; baptism at house 5, and on 18 August BORI&#262; JOSO was born at <strong>Kamenica house 23</strong> to BORI&#262; STIPE and Serti&#263; Luce &mdash; so that one house number now holds three Bori&#263; fathers (Tome, Alojz and Stipe), with three different wives (Mesi&#263;, Mesi&#263;, Serti&#263;), the same shape this page found at house 24. Serti&#263; is now the surname Bori&#263; women are found marrying into more than any other.</p><p><strong>A second child for the Tome Bori&#263; and Radoti&#263; Marija household at Kamenica house 24.</strong> BORI&#262; TOME was born 13 September 1899 &mdash; after Luce, in 1893 &mdash; which makes that household two children deep and reads as a real one beside the Niko Bori&#263; household at the same house, not a slip for it.</p><p><strong>A problem for a person to settle, not this page.</strong> This archive has treated a surname it reads as <em>Kalauj</em> as a distinct family since the first births item (a Kalauj Jure and Vu&#269;eti&#263; Ove, house 249, 1892). In March 1899 a <em>Kalanj</em> Jure and Vu&#269;eti&#263; Manole appear at house 231 with the same given names and the same mother's surname, and in this matičar's hand the two spellings cannot be reliably told apart by letterform. A KALANJ NIKO also stands witness to a baptism at Pla&#353;&#263;ica in September 1899. Nothing has been reclassified: the 1899 entries stay Kalanj because house 231 is Niko Kalanj's. But if the Kalauj family is Kalanj, this register holds more Kalanj entries than this page lists.</p><p><strong>And in November 1899 the house-154 Hofjenc household, behind the first Marija Bori&#263; this page named, gained a later child.</strong> HOFJEVAC IVE was born 12 November at Brinje house 154, son of Hofjevac Niko and Bori&#263; Marija, six years after Niko (1893); the husband's name and the house number tie him to that couple, not to the Vid Hofjevac of house 160. A BORI&#262; MILKA, a new given name, was a witness.</p><p><strong>November 1899 added Mesi&#263;, Serti&#263; and a new Kalanj.</strong> At Kamenica house 20, MESI&#262; PETAR was born 14 November to Mesi&#263; Stipo and <strong>BORI&#262; MARIJA</strong>, with another BORI&#262; MARIJA as witness &mdash; a Bori&#263; woman married out into Mesi&#263;, at the same time as Mesi&#263; women are the wives of three Bori&#263; men at Kamenica. At Brinje house 289, SERTI&#262; MANOLE was born 29 October to Serti&#263; Pave and <strong>BORI&#262; LUCE</strong>; the BORI&#262; MILKA who witnessed it stood witness again two weeks later at the Hofjevac baptism at house 154, both times beside a Hofjevac Niko or a Jela&#269;i&#263; Niko &mdash; probably one woman, probably a kinswoman of Marija Bori&#263;, not asserted. And at <strong>Brinje house 188</strong>, a second Kalanj household appears: KALANJ MANOLE, born 11 November 1899, daughter of Kalanj <em>Jerko</em> and Krnari&#263; Ru&#382;e &mdash; a new Kalanj father, at the house that already holds Viol Kalanj's third child, so two Kalanj households now share house 188.</p><p><strong>And house 249 sharpens the Kalauj question.</strong> KALANJ STIPE was born 18 November 1899 at Brinje house 249, son of Kalanj Mijo and Krnari&#263; Manda &mdash; the child of the 15 February 1892 marriage this page already holds, when Mijo, then about 53 and a widower, married a woman of 23. House 249 is also the house where a &laquo;Kalauj&raquo; Jure and his Vu&#269;eti&#263; wife were logged in 1892 as a distinct near-miss family. A house that holds a Kalanj bachelor in 1893, a Kalanj baby in 1899 and a &laquo;Kalauj&raquo; household in between is evidence for reading that household as Kalanj; it is recorded here as evidence, not as a correction, and a person should settle it.</p><p><strong>THE WHOLE FILM IS NOW WALKED.</strong> Image 489 is the film's own &laquo;END OF ITEM&raquo; card for the third births item (Matična knjiga ro&#273;enih 1897&ndash;1900), and the marriage book that follows was read earlier: <strong>births 1888&ndash;1900 and marriages 1888&ndash;1899 at Brinje, every image, from the first to the film's own end cards.</strong> The last births read added a third child to the Grgo Bori&#263; and Marija Milakovi&#263; household at house 126 (BORI&#262; MANOLE, 30 December 1899) and a Kalanj witness at house 228. Two things remain for a person, not for more walking: whether the near-miss <em>Kalauj</em> family is in fact Kalanj, and a second look at the near-miss witness decisions made at reduced zoom in the last stretch. Until then this register should not be called exhausted for Kalanj.</p><div class='note'><strong>THE KALAUJ QUESTION IS SETTLED: &laquo;Kalauj&raquo; is Kalanj.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>Re-read at full zoom on 5 October 2026, the entries this page had kept apart as a separate &laquo;Kalauj&raquo; family are plainly Kalanj: <em>Kalanj Marisa</em>, born 22 November 1891 at house 231 to Jure Kalanj and Vu&#269;eti&#263; Manole (the same parents and house as the 1899 Ru&#382;e); <em>Kalanj Marko</em>, 1892, at house 249; <em>Kalanj Ove</em>, 1894, at house 231; and a Kalanj Mile and Marija as witnesses at Lu&#269;ane in 1894. The earlier reading had defended its own misreading by matching each new entry to the last. <strong>It cost seven Kalanj entries never recorded</strong> &mdash; Marisa, Marko and Ove, and Petar (1891, Tome Kalanj and Nesi&#263; Marija, house 188), Pave (1892, Viol Kalanj and Kate Zori&#269;i&#263;, house 188), Manole (1895, Mijo Kalanj and Krnari&#263; Manda, house 249), and the Mile Kalanj witnesses &mdash; now on this page's list. <strong>Since done:</strong> images 305&ndash;318 and 351&ndash;355 were re-read the same day and gave more, set out in the next note.</span></div><div class='note'><strong>THE NEAR-MISS RETEST CHANGED THE ANSWER: &laquo;Perhoni&#263;&raquo; is Perkovi&#263;.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>Re-reading the near-miss stretches at readable zoom on 5 October 2026 found <strong>ten people the first pass had filed as &laquo;no new finds&raquo;</strong>. <em>Kalanj:</em> Luce, born on 25 December 1889 at house 231 to Jure Kalanj and Vu&#269;eti&#263; Manole, the eldest child of that household; Jure, born in September 1896 at house 249; and Mesi&#263; Manole, October 1896, whose mother was a Kalanj, Marija, with a Kalanj Manda as informant. <em>Bori&#263;:</em> mothers in a Karaka&scaron; birth and a Petrini&#263; birth of 1895; a Bori&#263; mother at a Serti&#263; birth of 1895; and a sixth child of Tome Bori&#263; and Jela at house 141, Kate (1896), whose own margin, read at full zoom, says she died on 2 April 1917 as a <em>Perkovi&#263;</em> wife. <em>Perkovi&#263;:</em> two households. The hand writes its <em>v</em> like an <em>n</em> &mdash; the groom's own name in the 1893 Vukovi&#263;&ndash;Perkovi&#263; marriage reads &laquo;Vukoni&#263;&raquo; on the same page &mdash; so the &laquo;Perhoni&#263;&raquo; this page had kept apart as a separate surname is the Perkovi&#263; surname. A 1895 Vukovi&#263; child (the same father, the same bride's surname and the same witness as the 1893 wedding) and the 1893 birth of Nikola, son of Mile Perkovi&#263; and Marica Perkovi&#263; at Kri&#382;polje 22, had both been filed as distinct. <strong>The earlier claim that every near-miss surname had been checked and kept distinct was wrong for this one.</strong> <strong>Not done:</strong> every other &laquo;Perhoni&#263;&raquo; sighting is reopened, so the number of Perkovi&#263; households on record is a floor; &laquo;Perki&#263;&raquo; is a visibly shorter word and is kept apart for now, though its bearer Mijo is an informant at Bori&#263; births in 1890, 1896 and 1899; Perhovi&#263;, Hori&#263;, &#272;ori&#263; and Verkovi&#263; were not retested; and most of the volume was read once at spread-fit zoom, where the first pass missed an entry in about every third image it was checked against. <strong>Four more followed from the same near-miss images:</strong> a Perkovi&#263; mother whose husband's own &laquo;Vukovi&#263;&raquo; is written &laquo;Vukoni&#263;&raquo; on the same line (1891), a second Perkovi&#263; wife in the Hofjevac family (1898), a Bori&#263; child of 1888 at Kamenica, and a Bori&#263; wife of Frane Vrani&#263; (1888). <strong>The full re-read, begun the same day, has now covered the whole of the first births book (images 10&ndash;194).</strong> It has found over a hundred more people (414 on record in all, after the third book below) and corrected the first pass's own rows: a house number (118 for 188), a month, a given name, and three households read as other surnames. It also <em>withdrew</em> a row this page's author had written, a Bori&#263; household at house 125 that is really <em>Beni&#263;</em>. Three Jure Kalanj households appear in 1890 (houses 231, 249 and 188) and cannot be one man. The second births book (1893&ndash;1897), on printed forms in a much clearer hand, has now been re-read to its end (images 198&ndash;400, 381 people on record), and it shows the first pass was not merely incomplete but sometimes wrong: months misread (a Bori&#263; baby born in January had been dated to February and another to August), a Javor child whose <em>mother</em> is a Bori&#263; filed as a mere witness, and a Lu&#269;ane birth whose mother is a <strong>Kalanj</strong> woman (Marija, wife of Ivan Marin&#269;i&#263;) read as another surname. One reading may explain a pattern: Javor Ivan, whose wife is a Bori&#263; and who stands as informant at so many unrelated births, is entered in 1893 as a <em>me&#382;njar</em> (sexton, as read), so his presence at those baptisms is weak evidence of kinship. At house 188 it found twin Kalanj boys, Ivan and Viol, born on 22 February 1896 to Viol Kalanj and Kate Zori&#269;i&#263;. Its last stretch adds a fifth child of the Jure Kalanj household of house 231 (September 1897) with a <em>Bori&#263;</em> man, Lojze, among the informants, and a Kalanj Marko at house 249 (November 1897); it also shows that the Javor family of house 83, with its Bori&#263; wife, cannot be read as one household, because three Javor Ivans with three differently named wives appear in 1896&ndash;97. <strong>The third births book (images 404&ndash;489, births of 1898, 1899 and the first days of 1900) has now been re-read at readable zoom as well</strong>, and adds 33 people to the file. Two first-pass Kalanj households held up and were confirmed at close zoom: Jerko Kalanj and Krnari&#263; Ru&#382;e at house 188 (a daughter, November 1899) and Mijo Kalanj and Krnari&#263; Manole at house 249 (a son, November 1899, seven years after Mijo&rsquo;s February 1892 marriage). It also found a Perkovi&#263; mother, Manole, wife of Mijo Juraji&#263; at house 286 (a daughter, November 1899; the hand writes <em>Perkoni&#263;</em>), and a long run of Bori&#263; women married into Mesi&#263;, Sertić, Hofjevac, Zori&#269;i&#263;, Karaka&scaron; and Petrini&#263; households. One first-pass mother was corrected from Marija to <em>Matija</em> (Mesi&#263; Stipo&rsquo;s wife at Kamenica), which joins her to the January 1898 child of the same Mesi&#263; household. The name <em>Perki&#263;</em> (Perki&#263; Mijo, a standing informant, and mothers named Perki&#263; at Vrani&#263; house 265 and Javor house 70) is recorded as read and is <em>not</em> silently called Perkovi&#263;. The Javor families are now at least four households, not one: Ivan with a Bori&#263; wife at house 83, Ive with a Župan&#269;i&#263; wife at houses 82&ndash;83, two Javor households with Perkovi&#263; wives at house 70, and a Mile Javor with a wife also born Javor at house 92; none is merged with another. The <strong>marriage book</strong> (one marriage to a printed page, images 493&ndash;690) is now being re-read the same way, and its first 159 pages (1888 to November 1892) have already <em>settled</em> three of the contradictions above. <strong>The two Javor Ivans of house 83 are father and son:</strong> a widower Javor Ivan, born about 1818, remarried on 18 November 1888, and on 15 September 1890 his son Javor Ivan, born at the same house, married Bori&#263; Ove of Kamenica, whose children fill the births of 1891&ndash;1898. <strong>The Mijo Kalanj wedding is 15 February 1892, not 1893</strong> &mdash; a misread digit &mdash; so the daughter Ane born in December 1892 was born ten months after it, and the supposed contradiction is gone; Mijo and Jure Kalanj of house 249 are sons of one Kalanj (the given name begins <em>Lju-</em>) and Ru&#382;e. <strong>The Niko Bori&#263; of house 142 who married in 1890 is the son of an older Niko Bori&#263;</strong>, and the Marija Bori&#263; who married Niko Hofjevac in 1892 is his sister. Jure Kalanj of house 231 married Vu&#269;eti&#263; Manole on 13 August 1888 (son of Vid Kalanj), and a Kalanj Marija of the same house, daughter of Joso Kalanj, married Ivan Marin&#269;i&#263; of Lu&#269;ane in 1892. In the marriage clerk&rsquo;s hand the name is written <em>Perhoni&#263;</em> and <em>Perkoni&#263;</em> &mdash; a Perkovi&#263; bride, Manole of house 115, in January 1891, and a Perkovi&#263; Niko as witness in 1889 and 1892 &mdash; while <em>Perli&#263;</em>, of house 101, is a separate name. <strong>The marriage book is now read to its last page</strong> (page 391, 31 December 1899). It holds only <strong>six Kalanj marriages</strong> &mdash; Jure of house 231 (13 August 1888, a son of Vid Kalanj), Jure of house 249 (19 November 1888), Tome of house 188 (19 November 1888), Niko of house 231 (16 February 1890, who married a widow n&eacute;e Vu&#269;eti&#263;), Marija of house 231 (24 January 1892, who married Ivan Marin&#269;i&#263; of Lu&#269;ane) and Mijo of house 249 (15 February 1892) &mdash; and none after 1892. The Perkovi&#263; are of two kinds: two sisters of house 115 (Manole 1891, Bare 1893, daughters of Jozo and Marija) and a Novi Kut/Kri&#382;polje line of Pere, Marko (1895), Niko (1897) and Petar (1899). Two more things the births could not say: <em>Perki&#263;</em>, the shorter word, is the <em>Perli&#263;</em> family of house 101, not Perkovi&#263;; and the Zori&#269;i&#263; wife who is &lsquo;Perkovi&#263; Kate&rsquo; in 1891 and &lsquo;Bori&#263; Kate&rsquo; in 1897 is two wives, because Pave Zori&#269;i&#263; was a widower when he married the Bori&#263; widow in September 1896. With both births books and the marriage book read at readable scale (461 people on record), this register can now be called exhausted for Kalanj, Bori&#263; and Perkovi&#263;; what it cannot do is connect any of these Brinje households to each other or to Josephus Kalanj of Klenovica &mdash; and the deaths volume, read the same day, adds the documented links and the one open conflict set out in the next section.</span></div>"],
      ["The deaths volume, read to its end", "<p>Read on 6 October 2026: DGS 005498263 (waypoint 9R2D-W32), Brinje's <em>Matična knjiga umrlih</em> 1888&ndash;1899, images 9 to 337, every printed page. <strong>48 deaths are on record, 23 Kalanj and 25 Bori&#263;, and not one Perkovi&#263;.</strong> The volume was read twice: first as whole spreads with every hit opened at readable zoom, then again page by page at readable zoom, both pages of every image. The second reading reproduced all 47 entries of the first and added one the first had missed, so the count is now a page-by-page count and no longer a floor; it cannot rule out a surname misread in an unusual hand, and the near-misses it opened and rejected (Karaka&scaron;, Rajkovi&#263; written Rajhoni&#263;, Crnkovi&#263;, Beni&#263;, Bosni&#263;) are named in the sources log.</p><p><strong>What it documents that the births could not.</strong> <strong>Janja Kalanj, a widow of house 231, died on 4 July 1889</strong>: Janja is the mother named on the 1888 marriage of Jure Kalanj, son of Vid and Janja, of house 231, so she is almost certainly his mother. <strong>The twin boys of Viol Kalanj and Kate Zori&#269;i&#263;</strong> (born 22 February 1896, house 188) <strong>died on 25 and 26 May 1897</strong>, at fifteen months. <strong>Ane Kalanj, 17, of house 231</strong> (d. 7 August 1892) is the sister of Marija Kalanj, bride of January 1892, by their parents' names, Joso and Ru&#382;e. <strong>Mijo Kalanj of house 249</strong> lost a wife, Bare, on 8 October 1891, four months before his second marriage (probable, because that entry gives no house). Children known only from a baptism are shown to have died young: Josip Bori&#263; at twenty days, Tome Bori&#263; of Kamenica 23 at two years three months, the first Marko Kalanj of house 188 at nine (1894), and the Jure and Marko of the two Vu&#269;eti&#263; households. <strong>Petar Kalanj, 3, died on 18 October 1894</strong> &mdash; the one entry the page-by-page reading added: almost certainly the Petar baptised in November 1891 to Tome Kalanj and Mesi&#263; Marija of house 188 (the death gives house 181, and the hand drifts by a digit), so that household lost him before the Manole of 1895 and the Franciska of 1898.</p><p><strong>The Bori&#263; houses, by their dead.</strong> House 141 lost four children of Ivan and Matija (Milka 1889, Kate and Vid on 19 and 20 December 1894, Marija on 28 December 1897). Kamenica 24 shows two couples: Tome and Marica (Marija at four in 1891, Luce in 1896, the second Marija at eight in 1899, the name given again each time) and Petar and Ru&#382;e (Josip at twenty days in 1891, Kate at nine in 1897). House 126 lost Mare, wife of Juraj, on 20 March 1890, which makes Juko Bori&#263;, married that July, a widower of four months if he is that Juraj.</p><p><strong>Probable, not proven.</strong> Two entries written <em>Galanj</em>, at house 151 (Mile, 74, d. 27 July 1895; Tome, 1&frac12;, March 1897), look like the household of the Marko Kalanj of house 151 and of the Mijo and Manole who were the parents of the bride Bare in 1893. <strong>A conflict left open.</strong> Viktorija, wife of Niko Kalanj of house 188, died on 4 December 1889, yet a Niko Kalanj of house 231 married on 16 February 1890 as a bachelor aged 48; either the status is a clerk's error or these are two men.</p><p><strong>The 1909&ndash;1910 emigrants, held against the registers.</strong> The three births, marriage and deaths volumes can say who <em>could</em> have been the men on the manifests, and who could not. <strong>Stipe Kalanj</strong> (b. 1885) was born before these books begin, and the only Stipe in them, baptised in November 1899 to Mijo Kalanj and Krnari&#263; Manda of house 249, is far too young. <strong>Mile Kalanj</strong> (b. 1889, aged 20 in 1909, wife Ana, met by an uncle Jure Bo&zcaron;i&#263;) has one candidate: the Mile born on 21 October 1888 to Tome Kalanj and his wife Marija at house 188, who does not appear among the deaths to the end of 1899. The age fits to within a year; the uncle's surname fits nothing in the household, and a marriage after 1899 is outside the filmed books, so this is a lead and not a match. The <strong>cousin Juro Bori&#263;</strong> who met Stipe has the same kind of candidate in Juraj Bori&#263;, born 3 February 1888 at house 141 to Tome Bori&#263; and Jela, alive through the deaths volume and 22 in 1910. Neither candidate is linked to anyone in the tree.</p><div class='note'><strong>WHAT THE DEATHS VOLUME DID NOT DO.</strong> <span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>No death entry noticed names Klenovica, Senj or America, and there is no Perkovi&#263; death to join the Kalanj and Perkovi&#263; marriage that the Gospi&#263; letter asks about. Nothing yet ties a Brinje Kalanj, Perkovi&#263; or Bori&#263; household to Josephus Kalanj of Klenovica or to anyone in the tree. With the births, marriage and deaths volumes of 1888&ndash;1899 all read, Brinje's filmed registers are exhausted for these three surnames at the level described here.</span></div>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p>Three things, in the order they would repay opening.</p><p><strong>1. Whether Brinje's own households join to each other &mdash; checked directly, 30 September 2026, and mostly not.</strong> The register has now put three separate Kalanj households at Brinje (houses 231, 249, 118), two separate Perkovi&#263; households (Novi Kut and house 145), and at least nine separate Bori&#263; households (houses 126, 141, 142, 191, and five more at Kamenica and Kri&#382; Kamenica) on this page. Within a household the register speaks plainly &mdash; a confirmed sibling pair at house 126, a confirmed sibling pair at house 141, and a likely father-and-infant-son at house 142 that sharpens rather than resolves a standing puzzle there. <strong>Across households, nothing joins them but the surname and the parish.</strong> No document has yet named two of these households as kin, and none of the roughly thirty Brinje Kalanj/Perkovi&#263;/Bori&#263; individuals found so far has been tied to Josephus Kalanj and Rozalija Perkovi&#263; themselves, or to anyone already in this tree. That may be the true shape of a small parish where three surnames simply run large, or, now that all three of Brinje's filmed 1888&ndash;1899 volumes are read, a gap these books cannot close: the Umrli (deaths), read to its end on 6 October 2026, names parents and survivors in one line and still joins no Brinje household to Klenovica or to the tree, so the join, if it exists, lies outside them.</p><p><strong>2. A death for Martin Kalanj</strong> at Novi Vinodolski or Klenovica in 1900, which would dispose of Trebinje without anyone going to Herzegovina.</p><p><strong>3. Which Marija is which</strong> in Luka and Kata's household at houses 36 and 37 in 1892 and 1894.</p><p><strong>And one thing that is known and is easy to lose sight of.</strong> This is the best-recorded family in the archive &mdash; 215 people, 87% of the records naming a place, every one of them joined to Hedviga. The hole is not in the evidence. <strong>It is forty kilometres inland, in a parish this archive has only just started to read.</strong></p>"],
    ],
  },
  boras: {
    eyebrow: "Senj",
    dek: "Fifty-five people, every one of them joined to Hedviga without scaffolding — and the least-researched family on this site. Not one Boras occupation is recorded anywhere in this archive.",
    body: [
      ["The family this archive has done least for", "<p>Two families here reach Hedviga <strong>entirely without scaffolding</strong>. Strip every sorting label out of the tree and <strong>all 55 Boras remain connected</strong> to her by ordinary parent, child and marriage links. Only <a href='/kalanj'>Kalanj</a> can say the same. Set that against <a href='/zubrinic'>&Zcaron;ubrini&cacute; at 5%</a> and <a href='/prpic'>Prpi&cacute; at 6%</a>.</p><div class='note'><strong>And nothing has been done with it.</strong> Thirty of the fifty-five carry no place at all, and <strong>not one Boras occupation is recorded anywhere in this archive</strong> &mdash; no <em>te&zcaron;ak</em>, no <em>nadni&ccaron;ar</em>, no trade, for fifty-five people across a hundred and twenty-six years.<span style='display:block;margin-top:.6rem'>The Senj registers that would fix both have been read, page by page, <a href='/papic'>for the Papi&cacute;i</a>. They have never been read for these people. <strong>It is the cheapest unread material in the archive.</strong> <a href='/buckets'>The scaffolding &rarr;</a></span></div>"],
      ["Where they are, and how they arrive", "<p>Seventeen of the thirty placed Boras records are at <strong>Senj</strong>, and the family runs from <strong>1820 to 1946</strong>. The connection is by marriage, twice: <strong>Franjo Boras</strong> (1888&ndash;1943) married <strong>Bo&zcaron;ica &laquo;Tonka&raquo; Papi&cacute;</strong>, and a second <strong>Franjo Boras</strong> (1914&ndash;1940) married <strong>Ru&zcaron;a Prpi&cacute;</strong>.</p><p>This page used to say they married into the Senj <em>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;i</em>. <strong>They did not.</strong></p>"],
      ["And a third marriage, which this archive only understood this week", "<p><strong>Angelika Boras married Martin Kosina</strong> &mdash; and until 27 September 2026 that was a line in a household list and nothing more. It is the join to <a href='/kosina'>the Carniolan Kosina</a>: Martin was born <em>unehelich</em> at <strong>Oberfeichting no. 9</strong>, near Kranj in Slovenia, on 16 November 1841, and the four generations above him were read out of the Kranj&ndash;&Scaron;martin registers this month.</p><p>So a Boras of Senj married into a Carniolan cottager's household that this archive can now follow back to a man born about 1775. <strong>And the marriage cost them two children in one year.</strong> The Senj death index carries <strong>Marija Kosina</strong> and <strong>Josip Kosina</strong>, both born and both buried in <strong>1896</strong>, both entered &laquo;Martin Kosina &times; Angjelina Kosina&raquo; &mdash; two infant deaths the family tree does not have, and probably twins, which the register would say and the index does not.</p><div class='note'><strong>That is the shape of the whole page.</strong> The Boras material is not thin because the family is obscure. It is thin because <strong>every hour this archive has had has gone somewhere else</strong> &mdash; and the one week it spent on their in-laws produced two children nobody knew about.</div>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. The Senj registers, for Boras.</strong> Baptisms, marriages and deaths, already in hand and already walked for other surnames. They would place thirty people, give the first occupation, and settle whether the two Franjos are kin.</p><p><strong>2. Angelika Boras's own marriage entry.</strong> It would name her parents and Martin Kosina's, and it is the one document that would tie the Senj Boras to the <a href='/kosina'>Kranj household</a> by evidence rather than by a tree line.</p><p><strong>3. Whether Marija and Josip of 1896 were twins.</strong> The index gives a year; the register gives a day.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  vukelic: {
    eyebrow: "Senj",
    dek: "The smallest family on this site — fifteen people — and the one that answers a question the archive asked for months: who Milka Papić married before Ljubomir Blažević.",
    body: [
      ["Milka's first husband", "<p><strong>Zvonimir Matej Vukeli&cacute;</strong>, born <strong>13 January 1883</strong>, died <strong>1915</strong> &mdash; married to <a href='/people/milka-lucia-papic'>Milka Lucia Papi&cacute;</a>, who is <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's mother</a>.</p><p>He is the reason <a href='/milka'>Milka was a widow at twenty-eight</a>, with a daughter of eight and a son of two, five years before she married Ljubomir Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;. <strong>Fifteen people are on this page and that one fact is why they are here at all.</strong></p><div class='note'><strong>His death entry has never been seen.</strong> 1915 is a year with no day, no place and no cause, in the middle of a war. A letter to Senj asking for it <strong>is drafted and unsent</strong>. <a href='/searched'>What has been searched &rarr;</a></div>"],
      ["Not “of Lika”", "<p>This page described them that way for the life of the site, and it was wrong. The published Vukeli&cacute;i are at <strong>Senj</strong> &mdash; ten of the twelve placed records &mdash; with single entries at <strong>Alan</strong> (house 8, 1823) and <strong>Mrzli Dol</strong> (house 25, 1900).</p><p><strong>Mrzli Dol is the <a href='/blazevic'>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; village</a></strong>, which is not a coincidence worth ignoring &mdash; a Vukeli&cacute; is at a Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; house number fifteen years before a Vukeli&cacute; widow married a Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;.</p>"],
      ["Three marriages, three families", "<p>For fifteen people they are unusually well connected. <strong>Michael Vukeli&cacute;</strong> married <strong>Stanislava Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute;</strong>; <strong>Marija Vukeli&cacute;</strong> married <strong>Josip &Zcaron;ubrini&cacute;</strong>; and Zvonimir married Milka Papi&cacute;.</p><p><strong>Three of the other eight families of this archive, out of a family of fifteen.</strong> A family this small touching that many is not a family on the edge of the story. It is a Senj household the tree has barely entered.</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Zvonimir's death, 1915.</strong> Day, place and cause all missing. The Senj death register for 1915, or the letter that is drafted and unsent.</p><p><strong>2. Whether the Senj stone is theirs.</strong> The <a href='/grave'>Bla&zcaron;evi&cacute; grave at Senj</a> stands among neighbours reading <strong>VUKELI&Cacute;</strong>, TOMLJANOVI&Cacute;, BEZJAK and ROGI&Cacute;. <strong>Nobody has checked whether that plot is this family</strong>, and one photograph of the inscription would settle it. <a href='/errands/#photographs'>What to photograph &rarr;</a></p><p><strong>3. Alan 8 in 1823 and Mrzli Dol 25 in 1900.</strong> Two houses, two records, seventy-seven years apart, and nothing joining either to the Senj ten.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  sestan: {
    eyebrow: "Gologorica, Istria",
    dek: "Fifty-five people whose entire connection to this family, when tested, runs through a single node the tree itself labels “not real”. They are kept, and every one of them is marked.",
    body: [
      ["Every route to them passes through a label", "<p>The shortest path from Hedviga to the nearest &Scaron;estan is <strong>eleven steps</strong>: out through Ivan Defranceski to his father Ante, to Josip de Franceschi, to Antonius, to Giovanni Battista, to <strong>Teresa Grach</strong>, to an unnamed spouse &mdash; and then across this:</p><blockquote class='note'><span class='mono'>Grah's for Investigation (not real)</span><span class='dek' style='display:block'>a node in the family tree, standing where a father should be</span></blockquote><p>&mdash; and on to <em>Gologorica Grah</em>, to Josephus Grah, to <strong>Rosa &Scaron;estan</strong>.</p><p><strong>Remove that one node and there is no path at all.</strong> Every one of the fifty-five becomes unreachable. They are published here because somebody was investigating whether they belonged, and <strong>the investigation is still sitting in the tree in the place where a father should be</strong>. <a href='/buckets'>Labels where the father should be &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["They are not where this site said they were", "<p>&Scaron;estan was described here as <em>&laquo;of the Croatian Littoral&raquo;</em>. Twenty-three of the twenty-four placed &Scaron;estani are at <strong>Gologorica</strong>, in central <strong>Istria</strong> &mdash; a different peninsula, eighty miles west of Senj, and nowhere near the Velebit littoral where every other family on this site lives. The remaining record is on <strong>Pag</strong>.</p><p><strong>And not one &Scaron;estan marries into any of the other eight families.</strong> Their marriages go to <strong>Defranceschi</strong> &mdash; three of them &mdash; and then to Depras, Andro&scaron;i&cacute;, Kri&scaron;mani&cacute; and Ga&scaron;pari&cacute;. Defranceschi is <a href='/hedviga'>Hedviga's husband's</a> family, not hers. <strong>So the geography and the marriages say the same thing the path says.</strong></p>"],
      ["What this archive is doing about it", "<p><strong>Keeping them, and marking every one.</strong> The <a href='/method'>method</a> here is to report the tree rather than edit it, and these are real records of real people at Gologorica between 1780 and 1947 &mdash; <strong>the doubt is about the <em>join</em>, not about them</strong>. Deleting fifty-five people to make a statistic look better would be the opposite of what this archive is for.</p><p>So all fifty-five carry a notice at the head of their own page saying that every route from Hedviga to them passes through a sorting label. A reader should not meet these names on a site about a woman from Senj without being told what connects them to her &mdash; and equally <strong>should not be told they are fictitious, because nothing here suggests that</strong>. <a href='/buckets'>The measurement &rarr;</a></p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Who Teresa Grach married.</strong> One name in one marriage entry would either replace the label with a man or prove there is nothing behind it. <strong>That single document decides whether fifty-five people belong on this site.</strong></p><p><strong>2. The Gologorica registers.</strong> Never opened. Istrian parishes are not in this archive's <a href='/searched'>reading plan</a> at all, because until the path was measured nobody knew the family was there.</p><p><strong>3. The Pag record.</strong> One &Scaron;estan, off on an island, joined to the other twenty-three by a surname.</p>"],
    ],
  },

  pavelic: {
    eyebrow: "Mrzli Dol, in the Senj hinterland",
    dek: "The family the Blaževići of Mrzli Dol kept marrying — twenty of the forty-three spouses recorded for Pavelić people are Blažević — with a branch that ended up at Hibbing, on the Minnesota iron range. None of them stands on the direct line; they are the in-laws, and this page says so.",
    body: [
      ["Not ancestors — the people next door", "<p>No Pavelić appears among Hedviga's ancestors in this tree, and this page does not pretend otherwise. They are here because of whom they married, and because there are so many of them: <strong>111 people</strong>, born 1815 to 1927, the great majority at <a href='/places/mrzli-dol'>Mrzli Dol</a>. Of the <strong>forty-three spouses</strong> the tree records for them, <strong>twenty are Blažević</strong> (one written <em>Blasevic</em>), eight are <a href='/prpic'>Prpić</a>, four are other Pavelići, three <a href='/kalanj'>Kalanj</a>, three <a href='/tomljanovic'>Tomljanović</a>, two <a href='/vukelic'>Vukelić</a> and one <a href='/krmpotic'>Krmpotić</a>. At Mrzli Dol the two names were one community marrying itself.</p>"],
      ["Four houses", "<p>The house is the unit that matters in these registers, as it is for the <a href='/prpic'>Prpići</a> at Krivi Put, and four numbers carry most of the family.</p><p><strong>House 14.</strong> <a href='/people/marko-pavelic-4'>Marko Pavelić</a> (1855–1932) married <strong>Ana Prpić</strong>, and the tree gives them children from 1878 to 1910 — Milan, Lucija, Marko, Krešimir, Zvonimir and a long run of children who died young (Kata, Mate twice, Franjo, Ruža, Josip, Danica, Matija, Anka). <a href='/people/zora-pavelich'>Zora</a>, who went to Minnesota, is entered as the daughter of a Marko Pavelić and an Ana Prpić — very probably this couple.</p><p><strong>House 30.</strong> <a href='/people/dujo-pavelic-2'>Dujo Pavelić</a> (1832–1908) married <a href='/people/antonia-tomljanovic'>Antonia Tomljanović</a>; Marija (1860), Mihovil or Mile (1862), Katarina, Ruža, Grga, Antonia, Ivan and Lucija are entered as their children, most of them at house 30, and Dujo died there.</p><p><strong>Houses 23 and 68.</strong> <a href='/people/martinus-dujo-pavelic'>Martinus Dujo Pavelić</a> (1853–1914) married <strong>Antonia Blažević</strong>. The tree puts most of their children at <strong>23</strong> — Marija (1873), Marta (1875), Mile Mihovil (1880), Marko (1882), Slavka (1898) — but Anka (1888) and Jure (1885) at <strong>68</strong>, Francisca (1877) at both at once, and Martinus Dujo himself at 68. That may be a household that split, or one register copied two ways; the tree cannot say.</p><p><strong>House 6 or 7.</strong> <a href='/people/ivan-pavelic-3'>Ivan Pavelić</a> (1851–1915) married <strong>Maria Blažević</strong>; their daughter Ana (1886–1965) was born at house 6 and married a Marko Pavelić, son of Mihovil — a Pavelić marrying a Pavelić.</p><div class='note'><strong>What this page cannot settle.</strong> Whether 23 and 68 are one house, and which of 6 and 7 is right, are questions for the Krivi Put births and the Senj parish books. They are not yet read for these children.</div>"],
      ["The marriages into the Blažević line, and a double", "<p>Four of the marriages show the pattern. <a href='/people/marija-pavelic-2'>Marija Pavelić</a> (1824 – before 1892), who died at Krivi Put, married <strong>Nikola Blažević</strong>; <a href='/people/antonija-pavelic-2'>Antonija Pavelić</a> (1836–1913) married <strong>Mihovil Blažević</strong>; the Marija Pavelić born 24 January 1862 married <strong>Mile Blažević</strong>; and <a href='/people/mile-pavelic'>Mile Pavelić</a> (1862–1939) of house 30 married <strong>Franjica Blažević</strong>. The tree carries that last man <strong>twice</strong> — as Mile, with a mother &ldquo;Ante Tomljanović&rdquo;, and as <a href='/people/mihovil-pavelic-2'>Mihovil</a>, with a mother Antonia Tomljanović — and the same for his son Jure (1890–1964, died at Barcs in Hungary). They are almost certainly one person each. This archive has not opened the registers for these marriages. The Krivi Put baptism index backs the first of them with a child: <strong>Mile Blažević</strong>, baptised 9 August 1854, son of Nikola Blažević and Marija Pavelić (<span class='mono'>sources/krivi-put-baptism-index.tsv</span>). It backs the third with another: a <strong>Petar Blažević</strong>, baptised in 1883 to <em>Mile Blažević</em> and <em>Marija Pavelić</em>, indexed as born 19 July where the tree says 19 June (the index misreads Croatian month names from 1878 on). The rest are the tree's claim, not a finding.</p>"],
      ["The first Pavelić this archive found in the Senj marriage book", "<p>In the Senj marriage volume (FamilySearch waypoint 9RK1-W3P) the earliest Pavelić read here is <a href='/found/thomas-pavelic-groom-1793'>Thomas Pavelić</a>, entry 167, <strong>April 1793</strong>, marrying a Magdalena, with the cathedral canon Philippus de Vukasović celebrating. He is a reading, not a branch of the tree: nothing joins him to the Mrzli Dol Pavelići, and the name is common enough that a join would need a baptism. A marriage of 21 October 1849 is read <em>Pavetić</em>; whether that is Pavelić misread is not decided. <a href='/snapshots/'>The page images are kept.</a></p>"],
      ["The ones who left", "<p><a href='/people/zora-pavelich'>Zora Pavelich</a> (27 December 1900 – 17 August 1990) was born at Mrzli Dol and died at <strong>Hibbing, St. Louis County, Minnesota</strong>; her parents are entered as Marko Pavelić and Ana Prpić (probably house 14) and her husband <strong>Ivan &lsquo;Ive&rsquo; John Pavelich</strong>, born Pavelić in 1893 and also dead at Hibbing. Their first child, <a href='/people/ann-sylvia-pavelich'>Ann Sylvia</a>, was born at Krivi Put in 1921; the next two, John (1923) and Mary Margaret (1925), at a place the tree calls <strong>Carson Lake (historical)</strong> in St. Louis County. So the move fell between 1921 and 1923 — an inference from three birthplaces, not a document.</p><p>Others went elsewhere. <a href='/people/michael-pavelich'>Michael Pavelich</a> (1876–1961), son of Ivan and Maria Blažević of house 6/7, died at Los Angeles; <a href='/people/matt-mate-pavelich'>Matt Pavelich</a> (1896–1938), Martinus Dujo's son, at Gold Park, Ontario; <a href='/people/marko-pavelic-6'>Marko</a> (1887–1968) at Sault Ste. Marie, Ontario; a Josip (1895) in the United States. At home the movement was to the city: Milan (1878–1938), Krešimir (1898–1960) and Slavka Sylvia (1898–1990) died at Zagreb. <a href='/america'>The other emigration</a> is where the rest of this archive's emigrants are collected.</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Where the Mrzli Dol Pavelići came from.</strong> The tree's earliest Pavelić is Josephus (born 1815, wife Anna Blažević), with no place; the earliest with a place is Marija, born 1824. The earliest with a register citation is Domnus, born 1847, son of Stephanus Pavelić and Ana Blasevic (Krivi Put births 1847–1884, image 6). The Krivi Put marriages before that, and the Senj baptisms, are the way back.</p><p><strong>2. The Mrzli Dol baptisms.</strong> The FamilySearch index of the Senj parish was swept on 14–16 October 2026 (<span class='mono'>notes/senj-baptism-index.md</span>). It turned up only <strong>two</strong> Pavelić children — Martina in 1766 and a child of a Pavi Pavelich in 1763 — and none from Mrzli Dol: the village was baptised at Krivi Put, and those are the books to open. The sweep does confirm one marriage in the tree: <strong>Ivan Juraj Prpić</strong>, baptised in October 1894, is the son of Petar Prpić and <strong>Antonija Pavelić</strong>.</p><p><strong>3. The doubles</strong> — Mile and Mihovil, the two Jures — are left as the tree has them, not merged; <a href='/duplicates'>the duplicates page</a> says how this archive treats a pair like that.</p>"],
    ],
  },

  tomljanovic: {
    eyebrow: "Karlobag, Senj and the canons",
    dek: "The one family on these new pages that reaches the direct line: Lucia (Maja) Tomljanović, ahnentafel 111, died at Karlobag in 1809. Nothing is known of her people beyond her father's name — and the Senj registers hold a good many Tomljanovići who cannot yet be joined to her.",
    body: [
      ["Ahnentafel 111", "<p><a href='/people/lucia-maja-tomljanovic'>Lucia (Maja) Tomljanović</a> is entered as born at <strong>Montani Stanište</strong> and died at <strong>Karlobag on 19 September 1809</strong>. She was the wife of Michael Gerkacs (1762–1836, ahnentafel 110) and the mother of <strong>Maria Gerkacs</strong> (1789–1834, ahnentafel 55), who married <a href='/karlobag'>Joannes Georgius Uroda</a> (born 1777, ahnentafel 54); their son Matia Uroda (1810–1886, ahnentafel 27) died at Senj. Her own father is <a href='/people/pauli-tomljanovic'>Pauli Tomljanović</a> (ahnentafel 222): the marriage entry of 2 November 1785 names him as <em>Paulus Tomljenovich of Montani Stanište</em>, and nothing else is known of him.</p><p><strong>Her age, and her children.</strong> The register of her death gives <em>annorum 34</em>, which cannot be right for a woman who married in 1785, and the page, re-read at full size, plainly says 34 (<a href='/snapshots/#karlobag-death-1809-lucia-maja-age'>snapshot</a>). The FamilySearch index shows the wife of Michael Gergacs named <strong>Lucia</strong> in five baptisms — Magdalena (1787), Maria (1789), Catharina (1794), Bartholomæus (1797) and Mathia (1801), four of them not in the tree. Either the figure of 34 is wrong, or Michael had a second Lucia. A read of the Karlobag marriage book (1789 to March 1801) found no marriage of a Michael Gergacs, and a read of the deaths book (1788 to January 1801) found no death of his wife — which favours one Lucia, but only weakly, since a second wife could have married in her own parish. A baptism of 1815 names a Michael Gergach and a wife <em>Martha</em>, so if that is the same Michael he married again after 1809 (<span class='mono'>notes/karlobag-gergacs-wife-search-1789-1801.md</span>).</p><p><strong>Her sisters.</strong> The same marriage book has a <strong>Barbara</strong>, daughter of <em>Paulus Tomljanovich of Stanište</em>, marrying the widower Joannes Medvidich on 27 July 1789 (<a href='/snapshots/#karlobag-marriage-1789-medvidich-tomljanovic'>snapshot</a>), and a <strong>Thoma</strong> (the given name is uncertain), daughter of <em>Paulus Tomljenovich of Stanište</em>, marrying the widower Philippus Gerpich of Ledenice on 25 May 1795 (<a href='/snapshots/#karlobag-marriage-1795-gerpich-thoma-tomljanovic'>snapshot</a>). The father's name and the hamlet are those of the bride of 1785, so they are probable sisters of Lucia; that is an inference, since Paulus was a common name and the hamlet had several Tomljanović households.</p>"],
      ["Two Pauluses at Karlobag in 1787", "<p>The Karlobag baptism index for 1787 holds <strong>two</strong> daughters of men named <em>Paulus Tomljanović</em>: <a href='/found/joannam-tomljenovich'>Joanna</a> (June), whose mother is written Thoma, and <a href='/found/matth-am-tomljenovich'>Matthaea</a> (7 October), whose mother is Catharina. They cannot be one household — two mothers — so either one man had two wives in four months, which is unlikely, or two men of one name lived at Karlobag. If either is Lucia's father, his daughter would be her sister. They are recorded here to keep the two apart, because &ldquo;a daughter of Paulus Tomljanović&rdquo; is not automatically a sister of <em>hers</em>. Both are indexed only; the register page has not been read. A further query on 17 October 2026 added a <em>Gregorius</em>, baptised 29 August 1782 to a Paulus Tomljenovich and a <strong>Catharina</strong>, so the Catharina household already had a child five years earlier; and it found <strong>no baptism of a Lucia or a Maja</strong> to any Tomljanović at Karlobag between 1740 and 1775 — which proves nothing, because the index paging is unreliable and Karlobag's earliest pages are thin.</p><div class='note'>This is the kind of near-miss this archive has been burnt by. <a href='/method'>The method</a> is to give each its own row and say what would join them, rather than choose the one that suits.</div>"],
      ["The canons of Senj", "<p>The Senj marriage volume, read page by page in October 2026, holds three canons of the name. <strong>Pop Pave Tomljanović</strong>, a titular canon, celebrates a marriage on 2 October 1739; <strong>Perica Tomljanović</strong>, a canon, in 1741; and <strong>Canon Petar Tomljanović</strong> in 1762 and 1764 — see <a href='/found/pave-tomljanovic-canon-officiating-priest-1739'>Pave</a>, <a href='/found/perica-tomljanovic-canon-celebrant-1741'>Perica</a> and <a href='/found/petar-tomljanovic-canon-celebrant-1762'>Petar</a>. Lay Tomljanovići stand beside them: Klara, daughter of Vule (bride, 18 February 1738), Antonia, daughter of Jule (bride, 14 May 1739), Pero (groom, 1742), Jure (groom, 1751), an Anton named in 1754. The surname is spelt <em>Tomjanovicha, Tommynovich, Tomlyanovich</em> by successive hands, and is old in Senj and its cathedral chapter. None of these has been joined to Lucia; the canons are of the name, not ancestors, and it is their lay relatives who would matter.</p>"],
      ["The death book", "<p>From 1820 the same volume is a death register, and the Tomljanovići in it are mostly small. Between 1834 and 1856 it records an infant of one day (February 1834), a boy of four months (1842), another of three months (1843), a girl of nine (December 1846), a girl of four (December 1847), and two boys of two or so, in November 1855 and February 1856. Among the adults is <strong>Georgius</strong>, a <em>Confiniarius</em> — a border-soldier — of Krmpote, aged 57, who died on 17 September 1855, and <strong>Matthaeus</strong>, a pensioned watchman of 54, who died on 2 June 1858. Georgius ties the name to the <a href='/frontier'>Military Frontier</a>.</p>"],
      ["Who the tree's Tomljanovići married", "<p>Twenty-six Tomljanovići are in the tree, born between 1831 and 1901. <a href='/people/antonia-tomljanovic'>Antonia</a> (1831–1897) married <strong>Dujo Pavelić</strong> and died at Mrzli Dol, house 30 — her children are the house-30 Pavelići (see <a href='/pavelic'>Pavelić</a>); <strong>Marija</strong> (1858–1940), born at Smokvica, married <strong>Pavao Blažević</strong>, and a Luce (1866) married a Nikola Blažević; <strong>Franjo</strong> (1870) married <strong>Amalina Žubrinić</strong> and lost a son, Živko, at four; <strong>Elias</strong> (1840, Krivi Put) married a Kalanj; <strong>Matijo</strong> (1876, born at Mrzli Dol, house 18) married a Papić, as did a Mandom. Three of the women married Prpići, and two Tomljanovići of the next generation married each other — Mihovil, born at Podbila in 1901 and dead in 1941, and Ana (1899–1978).</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Lucia's baptism and her parents' marriage.</strong> Judging by her daughter's birth in 1789 she was born about 1760–70. If Montani Stanište lay in the Karlobag parish, the Karlobag births of those years hold her (the register runs from 1727) and are still to be read for this name.</p><p><strong>2. Which Paulus.</strong> The two 1787 households need the register pages, not the index.</p><p><strong>3. Whether any Senj Tomljanović is an uncle or cousin.</strong> The Senj baptism index was swept on 14–16 October 2026 (<span class='mono'>notes/senj-baptism-index.md</span>): its earliest Tomljanović baptism is <strong>1820</strong> and nothing it found reaches back to Lucia, who was born about 1760–70 and died at Karlobag. What it does hold is a long run of Senj Tomljanović children from 1820 to 1894 and two households the tree already has: <strong>Ivan Tomljanović and Lucia Prpić</strong> (Matija, 1879) and <strong>Ivan Blažević and Marija Tomljanović</strong>, four of whose five children (1885–1894) are indexed at Senj.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  krmpotic: {
    eyebrow: "Senj, Krivi Put and Krmpote",
    dek: "A name that shares its stem with the villages above Senj and is written Krmpotich, Krampotich and Kermpotich by the clerks who recorded it. Seventeen readings from the Senj registers, nineteen people in the tree — and three St Salvator Krmpotići buried within nine years.",
    body: [
      ["Thirteen people, and a name from the mountain", "<p>The tree holds nineteen Krmpotići, born 1799 to 1894. The surname shares its stem with <a href='/places/krmpote'>Krmpote</a> and <a href='/places/krmpotske-vodice'>Krmpotske Vodice</a>, villages beside <a href='/places/smokvica-krmpotska'>Smokvica Krmpotska</a>, where the <a href='/blazevic'>Blaževići</a> came from. This archive has not established which word came from which, and one is not evidence of the other; a Krmpotić is not for that reason a man of Krmpote.</p><p>Two groups are well enough placed to describe. <strong>Veljun:</strong> <a href='/people/juko-krmpotic'>Juko</a> (1805–1880) and <a href='/people/ilija-krmpotic'>Ilija</a> (1815) were born at Veljun and, with Ivka below, are entered as children of the same &ldquo;Andrje or Dujo&rdquo;. Separately <a href='/people/ivan-krmpotic'>Ivan</a> (born at Veljun on 10 June 1890, died at Senj) married Paulina Boras; his parents are Juraj Krmpotić and Marija Pavelić, and the tree does not say how he relates to Juko and Ilija. The other is <strong>Antun Krmpotić</strong> of Krivi Put married <strong>Marija Prpić</strong> and had six children at Senj between 1881 and 1894 — Mile Vatroslav (born and dead in 1881), Anton Frane (1883, the same), Slavka Katarina (1885–1959), Marijan (1888–1912), Anton (1890–1934) and Leon Davorim (1894). All six have a baptism in the Senj index, the mother written Papić, Perpić or Prpić, and the index holds a <strong>seventh</strong>: <strong>Ivan Petar</strong>, baptised 27 August 1892 to <em>Anton Kermpotić</em> and <em>Marija Perpić</em>, who is not in the tree. A further child pre-dates the Senj years: the Krivi Put baptism index has an <strong>Ana Krmpotić</strong>, baptised 14 February 1878 to <em>Antona Krmpotić</em> and <em>Marija Perpić</em> (the index's months are not to be trusted, the year matches), and the Senj burial index records Antun's <strong>Leon</strong>, born 1894, as dead in 1896. <a href='/people/ivka-krmpotic'>Ivka Krmpotić</a> (born at Krivi Put on 23 June 1799, died 1833) married <strong>Marko Šojat</strong>; her father is entered only as &ldquo;Andrje or Dujo&rdquo; — the tree could not choose, and neither can this page.</p>"],
      ["St Salvator", "<p>The Senj death register of the 1820s and 1830s is the record that notices them. <a href='/found/maria-krmpotic-daughter-of-michael-of-st-salvator-died-senj-1825'>Maria</a>, daughter of <strong>Michael Krmpotić of St Salvator</strong>, died on 2 September 1825; <a href='/found/nicolaus-krmpotic-of-st-salvator-died-senj-march-1831'>Nicolaus Krmpotić of St Salvator</a>, a soldier-subject of the Frontier, on 15 March 1831; <a href='/found/anna-krmpotic-born-at-st-salvator-died-senj-february-1834'>Anna</a>, born at St Salvator, in February 1834; and <a href='/found/helena-krmpotic-of-st-salvator-bride-of-petrus-tomaic-1833'>Helena Krmpotić of St Salvator</a>, aged 17, married Petrus Tomaić, a soldier of Lukovo, on 4 February 1833. St Salvator is a place in the Senj parish (the register glosses it Sveti Spas). That the four belong to <em>one</em> family is likely and not proved: the register names a Michael as father of one and gives the others no father. The index adds two baptisms to a <em>Michael Kermpotich</em> — Lucia (25 September 1825, mother Mathia) and Maria Antonia (9 March 1834, mother Antonia Perpich) — which fit his marriage to a widow Antonia in 1830 but not the Stanislava of 1822, so he had at least two wives or there were two Michaels.</p>"],
      ["Michael Krmpotić, widower — twice", "<p>A <strong>Michael Krmpotić</strong>, widower of Senj, married a widow named Stanislava on 23 January 1822, and a Michael Krmpotić, widower of Senj, aged 54, married a widow, Antonia, on 18 May 1830 — <a href='/found/michael-krmpotic-widower-of-senj-groom-of-1822'>1822</a> and <a href='/found/michael-krmpotic-widower-of-senj-groom-of-1830'>1830</a>. It may be one man married three times, or two men of one name. The registers do not say, and the age and the eight years are not enough to decide. The rows are kept separate.</p>"],
      ["Krmpote, named", "<p>Two entries name the village. <strong>Margarita Krmpotić</strong>, a beggar (<em>mendicans</em>) of Krmpote, died at Senj on 20 September 1855, and <strong>Gregorius Krmpotić</strong>, a border-soldier (<em>Confiniarius</em>) aged 75, died on 8 January 1858 — see <a href='/found/margarita-krmpotic-mendicant-of-krmpote-died-senj-september-1855'>Margarita</a> and <a href='/found/gregorius-krmpotic-grenzer-aged-75-died-senj-january-1858'>Gregorius</a>.</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Whether Antun Krmpotić's line reaches the Senj registers of the 1830s.</strong> His Krivi Put origin is in the tree; his baptism is not.</p><p><strong>2. Whether the St Salvator Krmpotići are one family.</strong> The Senj baptisms for St Salvator would answer it.</p><p><strong>3. How much of this is a pedigree.</strong> It is not one: it is a gathering of readings, kept so that the next person who finds a Krmpotić in a Senj book can see what is already on record.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  babic: {
    eyebrow: "Senj — porters, masons and mariners",
    dek: "Thirty-five readings from the Senj registers of 1748 to 1858, and the one episode that stands out of them: seven Babić children dead between 31 July and 29 August 1855, four of them sisters in three days.",
    body: [
      ["Who they are in the tree", "<p>Fifteen Babići are in the tree, in three groups. The first: <a href='/people/anton-ivan-babic'>Anton &lsquo;Ante&rsquo; Babić</a> (born 1837 at Senj) married <strong>Tereza Prpić</strong> — see <a href='/prpic'>Prpić</a> — and had <a href='/people/marija-babic'>Marija</a> (1887), <a href='/people/ana-babic'>Ana</a> (1890–1962) and <a href='/people/mirko-babic'>Mirko</a> (1891–1938). His parents are entered as <strong>Augustin Babić</strong> and <strong>Maria Jurčić</strong>. A <strong>Kate Babić</strong> (and a <em>Catharina Babich</em> — probably the same woman) is recorded as the wife of a Stepan Žubrinić. The second group is <strong>Ivan Babić</strong>, who married first Kate Vukelić and then Karelina Skota; he is recorded with two wives — Kate Vukelić, mother of Jacov (1889), and Karelina Skota, mother of Ginro (1890) — and the two births are a year apart, so one attribution is probably wrong. Both boys were born at Senj, and Ginro married Antonija &lsquo;Tonka&rsquo; Blažević. The third is at <strong>Otočac</strong>: <strong>Joannes Babić</strong> and <strong>Helena Žubrinić</strong>, whose Antonius (born 12 October 1836) and Theresa (11 October 1845, at Otočac) are in the tree — a Babić household at Otočac, where the Žubrinići are. A Marijana Babić married a Josip Papić and a Roza a Blaž Glavičić. The register holds far more.</p>"],
      ["Ante's brothers and sisters", "<p>The register hands over Ante's family once you search by the father's name rather than the surname. <strong>Augustinus Babić</strong> and <strong>Maria Jurčić</strong> — written <em>Turcicz, Turcich, Turcsich, Furcić</em> and, in the 1856 marriage entry, <em>Farčić</em> — are named in the FamilySearch index of the Senj parish as the parents of eight children (<span class='mono'>sources/babic-augustinus-family-index.txt</span>): <strong>Hellena Francisca</strong> (baptised 28 March 1826), <a href='/found/joannes-babic-mariner-groom-of-1852-whose-mother-was-nee-jurcic'><strong>Joannes</strong></a> (23 June 1828), the twins <strong>Casparus</strong> and <strong>Paulus</strong> (4 January 1832 — the man of 1856 is entered as Casparus Paulus, so he carried both names or his twin did not live), <strong>Nicolaus Ambrosius</strong> (6 November 1834), <strong>Ante</strong> himself (baptised 17 May 1837, read from the page: <em>Joannes Antonius</em>, the son of a porter — <span class='mono'>notes/senj-baptism-1837-ante-babic.md</span>), <strong>Philipina Jacobina</strong> (24 April 1840) and <strong>Theresia</strong> (born 1842; the mason Augustinus's daughter who died at 14 on 20 December 1856, below). That settles two things this page used to leave open. The mariner Joannes of the 1852 marriage <em>is</em> Ante's older brother, and so is the mariner <a href='/found/casparus-paulus-babic-mariner-groom-of-1856'>Casparus Paulus</a> of 1856: his mother, written Farčić, is the same Maria Jurčić. There were not two or three Augustini Babić, only one.</p><p>The index also records their marriages, with the dates left out. <strong>Ante married twice</strong>: first <strong>Catharina Zoričić</strong>, daughter of Josephus Zoričić and Francisca Toljan — a wife the tree does not have — and then <strong>Tereza Perpić</strong>, daughter of Šimun Perpić and <strong>Ana Tomljanović</strong>, which ties him to the <a href='/tomljanovic'>Tomljanović</a> women of the tree. Children of the first marriage are indexed too — <strong>Vinko</strong>, baptised in 1878 to <em>Tone Babić</em> and Katarina Zoričić (and buried at Senj in 1883), and <strong>Antonija</strong>, born 1880, who died at Senj on 25 August 1902 — and a second Marija, born and buried in August–September 1888, to Tone and a Teresa, beside the Marija of 1887 the tree has. Joannes married Jacoba Fabris in 1852 and, later, <strong>Margaritha Pavelić</strong>, daughter of Dominus Pavelić and Anna Butorac. Casparus Paulus married a Maria Grandić, whose mother is indexed as a <strong>Catharina Žubranić</strong>, and later a Victoria Mihalitz; Nicolaus Ambrosius a Rosa Beszeg; Philipina Jacobina a Michael Hieronymus Lenacz; and a Hellena Babić (probably Hellena Francisca) a Joannes Basić.</p><div class='note'><strong>One thing to keep straight.</strong> A different Augustinus Babich, at <em>Babina Greda</em> in Hungarian Szerém, has children indexed 1835–1841 with a Maria Kedacsich. He is not ours and appears in the same search.</div>"],
      ["Summer 1855", "<p>The Senj death register for 1855 records a run of Babić deaths. On 20 July a Babić porter, <a href='/found/joannes-babic-porter-died-senj-july-1855'>Joannes</a>, died of dysentery, and the clerk wrote <em>cholera</em> beside it. Between 31 July and 29 August seven Babić children followed: <a href='/found/jacobus-babic-aged-4-died-senj-july-1855'>Jacobus</a> (4, a gardener's son) and <a href='/found/antonius-babic-aged-2-died-senj-august-1855'>Antonius</a> (2, a porter's), both of spasms; <a href='/found/josepha-babic-aged-10-died-senj-august-1855'>Josepha</a> (10, the porter Laurentius's daughter), of cholera; and <strong>four daughters of the porter Franciscus Babić</strong>, all of cholera — <a href='/found/maria-babic-aged-6-died-senj-august-1855'>Maria</a> (6) and <a href='/found/ursula-babic-aged-3-died-senj-august-1855'>Ursula</a> (3) on 27 August, <a href='/found/francisca-romana-babic-aged-4-died-senj-august-1855'>Francisca Romana</a> (4) on 28 August, and <a href='/found/maria-babic-aged-2-died-senj-august-1855'>another Maria</a> (2½) on 29 August. All were buried at S. Vitus.</p><div class='note'><strong>A caution.</strong> Two Marias of one father, aged six and two and a half, is either an unusual household or one child entered twice. The second entry itself mentions the first, which makes a double entry less likely and does not settle it. The two rows are kept apart until the page is read again.</div>"],
      ["The marriages", "<p>The marriage book adds <a href='/found/antonius-babic-of-senj-groom-of-1842'>Antonius</a> (23 January 1842, marrying Maria Kalanj, 20, daughter of Jacobus), <a href='/found/laurentius-babic-of-senj-groom-of-1842'>Laurentius</a> (6 February 1842), <a href='/found/franciscus-babic-of-senj-groom-of-1847'>Franciscus</a> (1847), <a href='/found/stephanus-babic-of-senj-groom-of-1848'>Stephanus</a> (6 March 1848), <a href='/found/christophorus-babic-mariner-groom-of-1851-with-a-bride-of-ledenice'>Christophorus</a>, a mariner (13 July 1851, a bride of Ledenice), and <a href='/found/casparus-paulus-babic-mariner-groom-of-1856'>Casparus Paulus</a> (1856). The earlier ones are in Latin: <a href='/found/katarina-babic-of-sv-juraj-bride-1748'>Katarina, daughter of Matiya Babić of Sv. Juraj</a> (29 April 1748), and a <a href='/found/mate-babic-groom-1766'>Mate</a> in February 1766. The trades the register gives are harbour trades — porter, mariner, mason, watchman, gardener — and a <strong>Georgius Babić</strong>, a veteran of a regiment, died in January 1855 aged 78.</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. The other Babić households of the cathedral book.</strong> Ante's baptism (17 May 1837, p. 25 entry 71 of the cathedral book, <a href='/snapshots/#senj-baptism-1837-ante-babic'>kept as a snapshot</a>) was read once the index had given his siblings; the index also holds his children Marija (1887) and Mirko (1891) and Jakov (1889), the son of Ivan Babić and Kate Vukelić (<span class='mono'>notes/senj-baptism-index.md</span>). Only one spread of the book has been read.</p><p><strong>2. Which Babić is whose.</strong> Ante's family is now settled; the Michael, Laurentius, Christophorus, Antonius and Franciscus Babić households of the 1830s–50s are not joined to it, and the 1855 cholera children are not placed.</p><p><strong>3. How the family reaches this one.</strong> Through Tereza Prpić, whose mother Ana Tomljanović is on the <a href='/tomljanovic'>Tomljanović</a> page, and through Joannes's wife Margaritha Pavelić.</p>"],
    ],
  },
  spalj: {
    eyebrow: "Sv. Jelena, Krmpote and Senj",
    dek: "Sixteen people born 1871 to 1894, four households — and in every one the wife is a Prpić. Beneath them, seven readings from the Senj registers going back to a lieutenant of Krmpote in 1747.",
    body: [
      ["Four households, four Prpić wives", "<p>The tree holds sixteen Špalji, and they fall into four households, each with the same shape. <strong>Daniel Špalj</strong> married <strong>Francesca Prpić</strong> and had Margaritte (1871, born at <em>Krmpote 152</em>), Stanislava (1873) and Mathea (1875, both at Senj). <strong>Josip &ldquo;Joso&rdquo; Špalj</strong> married <strong>Ana Prpić</strong> and had Kate (1886), Marija Pepica (1888), Jure Milkan (1891) and <a href='/people/lucija-spalj-spalj'>Lucija</a> (14 January 1894, at Sv. Jelena, who married Ivan Belobarbić). <strong>Franjo Špalj</strong> married <strong>Marta Prpić</strong> and had Ana (1887) and Martin (1893, at <em>Sv. Jelena, Krivi Put 130</em>). <strong>Mate Špalj</strong> married <strong>Matija Prpić</strong> and had Mile (1888, <em>Sv. Jelena Br 152</em>) and Klara (1893). Separately <a href='/people/lucia-spalj'>Lucia Špalj</a> is the wife of Josip Prpić. That is five Prpić marriages with a Špalj in the tree.</p><p>Two things are worth saying plainly. The tree does not name the four men's parents, so <strong>whether they were brothers is not known</strong>. And the Krmpote and Sv. Jelena places both carry the number <strong>152</strong> — Margaritte in 1871 and Mile in 1888, in two different households — which is either one house, or one number written twice; the parish register would say.</p>"],
      ["Under them: the Senj registers", "<p>The Senj marriage-and-death volume holds seven more, and they reach back a century and a quarter before the sixteen. The earliest is <a href='/found/matia-spalj-lieutenant-of-krmpote-groom-1747'><strong>Gospodin Matia Špalj</strong>, a lieutenant of Krmpote</a>, who married at Senj on 28 May 1747 — the rank (<em>lajtmanta</em>) and the title <em>gospodin</em> are a military and a social mark, and he is the only Špalj in these pages with either. The deaths are old: <a href='/found/joannes-spalj-spal-aged-80-died-senj-1820'>Joannes</a>, about 80, in 1820; another Joannes on 27 November 1828; <a href='/found/hellena-spalj-aged-79-died-senj-november-1831'>Hellena</a>, 79, in November 1831; <a href='/found/catharina-spalj-widow-aged-78-died-senj-august-1836'>Catharina</a>, a widow of 78, in August 1836. By their ages they were born about 1740, 1752 and 1758, a generation or so after the lieutenant. The younger readings are marriages: <a href='/found/maria-spalj-bride-1814'>Maria</a> in January 1814 and <a href='/found/francisca-spalj-bride-of-malik-1820'>Francisca</a>, who married Joannes Malik on 2 July 1820.</p><div class='note'><strong>The bridge, half-built.</strong> The Senj baptism index (<span class='mono'>notes/senj-baptism-index.md</span>) holds 31 Špalj children, 1822 to 1894, and the 1850s ones reach toward the 1870s: <strong>Daniel Špalj and Franciscaina Perpić</strong> baptise a daughter on 1 December 1854 and another child on 22 February 1857 — the same wife's name as the tree's Daniel, whose children are born 1871–75; Ilija Špalj and Lucija Perpić baptise three (1851–57); Lovre Špalj and Marie Perpich one (1849). Prpić or Perpić wives recur from 1849. The index shows the families; it does not say who was whose son.</div><p>The Krivi Put baptism index (<span class='mono'>sources/krivi-put-baptism-index.tsv</span>, 17 October 2026) shows that <strong>Špalj is above all a Krivi Put name</strong>: 136 Špalj records from 1847 to 1884, among them Špalj fathers with Perpić, Tomljanović, Butković, Šojat and Vukelić wives. A Daniel-and-Perpić household may turn up there too — a <em>Michael</em> baptised on 4 September 1859 to a father indexed as &ldquo;Donieb Špalj&rdquo; (the forename is garbled, possibly Daniel) and Franciscae Perpić — which would mean the household moved between Senj and Krivi Put in the 1850s. Franjo Špalj and Marta Prpić's first child in the index, <em>Juraj</em>, was baptised at Krivi Put in 1884, three years before Ana at Senj. And a <em>Joso Špalj</em>, baptised 2 February 1855 to Mate Špalj and Anne Perpić, is a candidate for the tree's Josip &lsquo;Joso&rsquo; — nothing but the name and the Perpić mother says so.</p>"],
      ["What is not known, and what would answer it", "<p><strong>1. Whether Daniel, Josip, Franjo and Mate were brothers.</strong> Their own baptisms, between about 1845 and 1865, are not in the index under these names; the Senj baptisms of those years, read from the page, are the way to see who their fathers were.</p><p><strong>2. What house 152 is.</strong> The Krmpote and Sv. Jelena registers.</p><p><strong>3. Whether the two Joannes</strong> (died 1820 aged about 80, and 1828 with no age) are one man or father and son.</p>"],
    ],
  },
};
